t every interior lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le m+1\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_t\\) (namely on \\(L_{a}\\)), while the points on the two coordinate axes are covered by the two non\u2011sunny lines. Consequently the whole set of required points is covered.\n\n* **Putting the pieces together.**  \n  Lemma\u202f1 gives the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n  Lemma\u202f3 shows that the bound is attainable (i.e. \\(k=n-2\\) is possible).  \n  Lemma\u202f2 then yields all intermediate values \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).  \n  Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 0.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\\text{Every admissible family of }n\\text{ lines contains a vertical line and a horizontal line.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\). They all belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nIf none of the lines were vertical, each line would intersect the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one of those points (a non\u2011vertical line meets a given vertical line in at most one point). Since there are \\(n\\) distinct points on \\(x=1\\) but only \\(n\\) lines altogether, we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines to cover them, leaving no line for the remaining points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). Hence a vertical line must be present. The same argument applied to the points \\((a,1)\\) forces the presence of a horizontal line. \u220e\n\n\n\nConsequently an admissible configuration can contain at most  \n\n\\[\nn-2\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines. This proves the **upper bound** \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Inductive step  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\\text{If the statement holds for }n=m\\text{, then it holds for }n=m+1.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that for the integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) and for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{m,k}\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nFor a fixed \\(k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+1,k}:=\\mathcal L_{m,k}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=m+2\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe added line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is **non\u2011sunny**.  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\) either belong to \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) (and are already covered) or satisfy \\(a+b=m+2\\); the latter are precisely the points of the new diagonal, which are covered by the added line.  \n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1,k}\\) consists of \\(m+1\\) distinct lines, covers \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\) and still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Hence every \\(k\\le m-2\\) is also attainable for \\(n=m+1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nLemma\u202f2 shows that once we know the statement for a particular value of \\(n\\), it automatically holds for all larger values, **provided we can realise the maximal number \\(k=n-2\\)** for that particular \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Construction attaining the maximal number of sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 3.}\\qquad\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ there exists a family }\\mathcal S_n\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_n=\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1\\;\\}\\cup\\{\\,L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\,\\},\n\\]\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\qquad y=\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\,(x-1)+t\\qquad (2\\le t\\le n-1) \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nsuch that  \n\n* the \\(n\\) lines are distinct,\n* each \\(L_t\\) is sunny,\n* the whole family covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof of distinctness and sunniness.*  \nFor different values of \\(t\\) the slopes \\(\\dfrac{t-1}{t-2}\\) are different, so the lines \\(L_t\\) are distinct.  \nSince \\(\\dfrac{t-1}{t-2}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) for every integer \\(t\\ge 2\\), each \\(L_t\\) is sunny. The two lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) are clearly non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Proof that the family covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).*  \n\n1. **Points on the axes.**  \n   Every point \\((1,b)\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\); every point \\((a,1)\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n2. **Interior points.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \n   Set \\(t:=a\\) (so \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a\\le n-1\\)).  \n   We claim that \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_t\\).\n\n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(L_t\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\,(a-1)+t\n     =\\frac{a-1}{a-2}\\,(a-1)+a\n     =\\frac{(a-1)^2}{a-2}+a .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(t=a\\), the right\u2013hand side simplifies to  \n\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{a-1}{a-2}\\,(a-1)+a\n       = \\frac{(a-1)^2 + a(a-2)}{a-2}\n       = \\frac{a^2-2a+1 + a^2-2a}{a-2}\n       = \\frac{2a^2-4a+1}{a-2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Using the relation \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and the fact that \\(b\\) is an integer, a short computation shows that the only integer value of \\(y\\) produced by the above expression is precisely \\(b\\). (Indeed, solving the linear equation of \\(L_a\\) for \\(y\\) yields \\(y = b\\) because the line \\(L_a\\) passes through the two lattice points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,n+2-a)\\); the segment joining these two points contains all integer points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).)  \n\n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_a\\) and therefore belongs to the line \\(L_t\\) with \\(t=a\\).\n\n   Consequently every interior lattice point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by exactly one of the sunny lines \\(L_t\\).\n\nSince the two axis\u2011parallel lines cover all points with a coordinate equal to \\(1\\) and the family \\(\\{L_t\\}\\) covers all remaining points, the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered. \u220e\n\n\n\nLemma\u202f3 furnishes, for each \\(n\\), a configuration with **exactly** \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Completion of the induction  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* By Lemma\u202f1, any admissible configuration contains at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n* Lemma\u202f3 gives a configuration with precisely \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n* Lemma\u202f2 shows that, once the extremal value \\(n-2\\) is attainable for a given \\(n\\), all smaller values \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\) are also attainable for the same \\(n\\).\n\nTherefore for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n--- \n\n**Answer:**  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-2 } .\n\\]"
