e \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Inductive propagation (Lemma\u202f3).**  \n   Assume that for some \\(m\\ge 3\\) and for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m-2\\) a suitable configuration of \\(m\\) lines exists.  \n   Adding the non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=m+2\\) to such a configuration yields a configuration of \\(m+1\\) lines that still contains exactly the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines and now covers all points required for \\(n=m+1\\). Consequently, once the extremal value \\(k=n-2\\) is attainable for a given \\(n\\), all smaller values \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\) are automatically attainable for the same \\(n\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 gives the universal upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Lemma\u202f2 shows that the bound is sharp (the value \\(k=n-2\\) is realizable).  \n   Lemma\u202f3 then yields every intermediate value \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).  \n\nHence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 0.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Two non\u2011sunny lines are unavoidable  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\\text{Every admissible family of }n\\text{ lines contains a vertical line and a horizontal line.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); they all belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nIf none of the lines were vertical, each line could meet the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one of those points, so at most \\(n\\) points could be covered by the whole family. Since the points \\((1