ns exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof that \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).*  \n\n1. **Points with a coordinate equal to \\(1\\).**  \n   Every point \\((1,b)\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\); every point \\((a,1)\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n2. **Interior points.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \n\n   *If \\(a\\neq\\frac{n+2}{2}\\)*, then by (3) the line \\(L_a\\) passes through the two points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,n+1-a)\\).  \n   The segment joining these two points consists of all points \\((x,y)\\) with \\(x\\) ranging from \\(1\\) to \\(a\\) and \\(y\\) ranging linearly from \\(n+1-a\\) down to \\(1\\). Since \\(b\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on that segment, hence on \\(L_a\\).\n\n   *If \\(a=\\frac{n+2}{2}\\) (this can happen only when \\(n\\) is even)*, then by definition we used the second case of (3); the line \\(L_a\\) now passes through \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,n-a)\\).  \n   Because \\(a=\\frac{n+2}{2}\\) we have \\(n-a = \\frac{n}{2}\\). The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(b\\le \\frac{n}{2}=n-a\\). Hence again \\(1\\le b\\le n-a\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the segment joining \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,n-a)\\), i.e. on \\(L_a\\).\n\n   In both cases the interior point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by the line \\(L_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nLemma\u202f2 shows that the upper bound (2) is sharp: the value \\(k=n-2\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive propagation of smaller values  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 3.}\\qquad\\text{If a configuration for }n=m\\text{ exists with }k\\text{ sunny lines, then a configuration for }n=m+1\\text{ exists with the same }k.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\