nny.  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   T:=\\{\\,t\\mid -(n-m-1)\\le t\\le n-m-1\\,\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains exactly \\(2(n-m)-1\\) distinct intercepts, more than enough to assign a different \\(\\ell_t\\) to each point of the omitted diagonals, because for a point \\((a,b)\\) on a diagonal \\(d_c\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b-a=c-2a\\in\\{-(c-2),-(c-4),\\dots ,c-2\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a set of size \\(c-1\\le n-m\\).  \n   Hence we can choose **exactly** \\(n-m\\) distinct lines among the \\(\\ell_t\\)\u2019s, one for each of the \\(n-m\\) remaining lines, and arrange that every point on an omitted diagonal lies on the line whose intercept equals its difference \\(b-a\\).  \n   Because each \\(\\ell_t\\) meets every diagonal in at most one point, no two points are forced onto the same line, and the lines remain distinct.\n\n4. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   The construction uses  \n\n   * \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny diagonal lines \\(d_{n+2-m},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\);\n   * \\(n-m\\) sunny lines of the form \\(y=x+t\\).\n\n   Hence the number of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-m .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(m\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), the attainable values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\nThe remainder of the answer contains the complete rigorous proof of the statements sketched above.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nd_c:=\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=c\\}\\;(2\\le c\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\nFor a line \\(L\\) we call it **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA line that is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions meets a fixed diagonal \\(d_c\\) in at most one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line \\(L\\) met \\(d_c\\) in two distinct points of \\(S_n\\), then the segment joining those two points would have slope \\(-1\\); consequently \\(L\\) would be parallel to \\(d_c\\), contradicting the hypothesis. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nLet \\(D\\subset\\{d_2,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\\) be the set of diagonal lines that we keep in our family.  \nIf \\(|D|=m\\) and the remaining \\(n-m\\) lines are arbitrary (sunny or parallel to the axes), then for every omitted diagonal \\(d_c\\) we must have  \n\n\\[\nc-1\\le n-m .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe diagonal \\(d_c\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nSince none of the \\(n-m\\) remaining lines is parallel to \\(d_c\\), Lemma\u202f1 shows that each of those lines can contain at most one of the \\(c-1\\) points.  \nHence we need at least \\(c-1\\) distinct lines among the \\(n-m\\) to cover all points of \\(d_c\\), which yields (2). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nCondition (2) can hold for all omitted diagonals only when the omitted diagonals are precisely  \n\n\\[\nd_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1-m}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a diagonal \\(d_c\\) with \\(c>n+1-m\\) were omitted, then \\(c-1\\ge n-m+1\\), contradicting (2).  \nConversely, for every \\(c\\le n+1-m\\) we have \\(c-1\\le n-m\\), so (2) is satisfied. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Construction of the sunny lines)  \nFix an integer \\(m\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le n\\) and keep the \\(m\\) largest diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_m:=\\{d_{n+2-m},d_{n+3-m},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nT:=\\{\\,t\\in\\mathbb Z\\mid -(n-m-1)\\le t\\le n-m-1\\,\\},\n\\qquad \n\\ell_t:\\;y=x+t\\;(t\\in T).\n\\]\n\nThen we can select exactly \\(n-m\\) distinct lines among the \\(\\ell_t\\)\u2019s such that every point of \\(S_n\\) lying on a diagonal not belonging to \\(D_m\\) lies on one of the selected lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider