one point, and together they cover all required points.\n\nThe remaining values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) still require a full construction; the difficulty lies in arranging exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines so that every point on the omitted (small) diagonals is hit, while respecting the \u201cat most one point per diagonal\u201d restriction of Lemma\u202f1.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nd_c:=\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=c\\}\\;(2\\le c\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is sunny, then for any fixed diagonal \\(d_c\\) the set \\(L\\cap d_c\\cap S_n\\) contains at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(L\\) contained two distinct points of \\(d_c\\), then the segment joining them would have slope \\(-1\\); consequently \\(L\\) would be parallel to \\(d_c\\), i.e. have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nLet a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nIf exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, then every diagonal that is **not** among the non\u2011sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nc-1\\le k .\\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a diagonal \\(d_c\\) that is not represented by a non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nThe diagonal contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nSince none of the \\(k\\) sunny lines is parallel to \\(d_c\\), Lemma\u202f1 implies that each sunny line can contain at most one of those points.  \nHence we need at least \\(c-1\\) distinct sunny lines, i.e. (\u2605). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------