ny lines is parallel to \\(d_c\\), Lemma\u202f1 implies that each sunny line can contain at most one of those points.  \nHence we need at least \\(c-1\\) distinct sunny lines, i.e. (\u2605). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nCondition (\u2605) can hold for all omitted diagonals only when the omitted diagonals are precisely  \n\n\\[\nd_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{\\,n+1-k}.\n\\tag{\u2020}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a diagonal \\(d_c\\) with \\(c>n+1-k\\) were omitted, then \\(c-1\\ge n+1-k\\) which contradicts (\u2605) because the right\u2011hand side equals \\(k\\).  \nConversely, for every \\(c\\le n+1-k\\) we have \\(c-1\\le k\\), so (\u2605) is satisfied. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence (necessary condition)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we see that the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\); no other restriction follows from the covering requirement. Thus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n}\n\\]\n\nis a **necessary** condition for the existence of a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Partial constructions\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nd_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1},\n\\]\n\nall of slope \\(-1\\).  Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of these diagonals, so the covering condition is fulfilled and no line is sunny.\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=x+i-1\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(1\\) (hence is sunny) and meets any diagonal \\(d_c\\) in at most one point (Lemma\u202f1).  Since there are \\(n\\) diagonals and each diagonal contains at most \\(n\\) points, the collection \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Remarks on the remaining 