"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nare exactly the non\u2011negative integers for which one can find \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.  \nIn other words, all values \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable **except** \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Step\u202f1 \u2013 Notation and elementary observations.*  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we write  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions\n\\(\\ell_x\\!:\\;y=0\\), \\(\\ell_y\\!:\\;x=0\\) or \\(\\ell_d\\!:\\;x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n*Step\u202f2 \u2013 Upper bound for the number of points on a sunny line.*  \n\n> **Lemma\u202f1.**  \n> Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lying on \\(L\\) have pairwise distinct\n>  \u2013 abscissas,\n>  \u2013 ordinates,\n>  \u2013 and sums \\(a+b\\).  \n> Consequently \\(|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  If two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on \\(L\\) had the same abscissa, the line would be vertical,\nhence parallel to \\(\\ell_y\\); similarly for equal ordinates (horizontal) and equal sums (parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\)).\nSince a sunny line is not parallel to any of these three directions, the three quantities must be different.\nThe possible values of each quantity belong to the sets \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)) and\n\\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) (for \\(a+b\\)), each of size \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 Impossibility of exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.*  \n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line exists.\nWithout loss of generality the non\u2011sunny line is horizontal, say \\(y=b_0\\) (the other two cases are analogous).\n\nThe row \\(y=b_0\\) contains \\(n+1-b_0\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and is completely covered by the horizontal line.\nConsider any other row \\(y=b\\;(b\\neq b_0)\\).  \nA sunny line can meet this row in **at most one** point (otherwise it would be horizontal).  \nHence the total number of points that can be covered in the row \\(y=b\\) by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines is at most \\(n-1\\).\n\nBut the row \\(y=1\\) (or, if \\(b_0=1\\), the row \\(y=2\\)) contains **\\(n\\)** points, while \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can supply at most \\(n-1\\) of them.  \nThus at least one point of that row would remain uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line (i.e. with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines) cannot exist.\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 Constructions for all admissible values of \\(k\\).*  \n\nWe give explicit families of lines for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq n-1\\).\n\n*   **\\(k=0\\).**  \n    Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n    \\[\n    L_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n    \\]\n\n    Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) has ordinate \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and therefore lies on \\(L_b\\).\n\n*   **\\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n    Let  \n\n    \\[\n    S_j:\\;y=x+j\\qquad (j=0,1,\\dots ,k-1)\n    \\]\n\n    be \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\).  \n    The line \\(S_j\\) contains the points \\((t,t+j)\\) for all integers \\(t\\) with\n    \\(1\\le t\\le n-j\\); in particular it covers exactly \\(n-j\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n    The remaining points are those whose ordinate belongs to the set  \n\n    \\[\n    B=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,j+1\\mid 0\\le j\\le k-1\\,\\}.\n    \\]\n\n    There are \\(n-k\\) such ordinates.  For each \\(b\\in B\\) draw the horizontal line  \n\n    \\[\n    H_b:\\;y=b .\n    \\]\n\n    The collection  \n\n    \\[\n    \\{S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\}\\cup\\{H_b\\mid b\\in B\\}\n    \\]\n\n    consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the first \\(k\\) are sunny,\n    and together they cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*   **\\(k=n\\).**  \n    Enumerate the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) as  \n\n    \\[\n    P_i=(i,\\,n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n    \\]\n\n    For each \\(i\\) define the sunny line  \n\n    \\[\n    L_i:\\;y=-\\frac{1}{i-1}\\,x+\\frac{n+1}{i-1}\\qquad (i\\ge 2),\\qquad \n    L_1:\\;x=1 .\n    \\]\n\n    (For \\(i=1\\) we simply take the vertical line \\(x=1\\); for \\(i\\ge 2\\) the slope is\n    \\(-\\frac{1}{i-1}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\), so the line is sunny.)  \n    One checks that \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n    \\[\n    (i,\\,1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (1,\\,i),\n    \\]\n\n    both belonging to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  As \\(i\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\) the \\(n\\) lines\n    \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (each point \\((a,b)\\) appears on the line\n    \\(L_a\\) or on \\(L_b\\)).  All \\(n\\) lines are sunny, giving a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k\\) we have exhibited a concrete set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \n\\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \n\\ell_d:\\;x+y=0,\n\\]\n\nand **sunny** otherwise.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf \\(L\\) is a sunny line, then the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lying on \\(L\\) have pairwise distinct\nabscissas, ordinates and sums \\(a+b\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in L\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfy \\(a_1=a_2\\).\nThen the segment joining them is vertical, so \\(L\\) is vertical, i.e. parallel to \\(\\ell_y\\),\ncontradicting the assumption that \\(L\\) is sunny.  The same argument excludes equal\nordinates (horizontal) and equal sums (parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\)).  Hence the three quantities are\npairwise different among the points of \\(L\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\).  The possible values of each\nquantity belong to a set of size \\(n\\) (\\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) for\n\\(a+b\\)).  Therefore at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) can lie on a sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncovering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\nHence \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains precisely one non\u2011sunny line.\nBy symmetry we may suppose that this line is horizontal, say  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=b_0\\qquad (1\\le b_0\\le n).\n\\]\n\nThe row \\(y=b_0\\) contains \\(n+1-b_0\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and all of them are covered by \\(H\\).\n\nConsider any other row \\(y=b\\) with \\(b\\neq b_0\\).  \nA sunny line cannot be horizontal; therefore it meets the row \\(y=b\\) in **at most one**\npoint.  Since there are only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, at most \\(n-1\\) points of the row \\(y=b\\) can be\ncovered by the sunny lines.\n\nNow look at the row \\(y=1\\).  \nIf \\(b_0\\neq 1\\) then this row is not covered by the horizontal line \\(H\\); it contains exactly\n\\(n\\) points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  The \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can supply at most\n\\(n-1\\) of them, leaving at least one point uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\nIf \\(b_0=1\\) then the uncovered row with the largest number of points is \\(y=2\\), which\ncontains \\(n-1\\) points.  In this case the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines could, in principle, cover all\nthose points, but we must also cover the row \\(y=3\\), which contains \\(n-2\\) points, and so on.\nSumming the maximal numbers of points that the sunny lines can contribute to each of the\n\\(n-1\\) rows \\(y=2,\\dots ,n\\) gives at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-2)+\\dots +1=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.  Together with the \\(n\\) points of the row \\(y=1\\) already covered by \\(H\\) this yields at\nmost  \n\n\\[\nn+\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}2- \\frac{n}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is strictly fewer than \\(|\\mathcal P_n|\\).  Hence some point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) would remain\nuncovered, again a contradiction.\n\nThus a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line (i.e. with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines) cannot\nexist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions for all other values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq n-1\\), a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) has ordinate \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and therefore lies on\n\\(L_b\\).  All lines are parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\), hence non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nDefine the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\;y=x+j\\qquad (j=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_j\\) has slope \\(1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it is sunny.\nFor a fixed \\(j\\) the integer points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on \\(S_j\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(t,t+j)\\mid 1\\le t\\le n-j\\},\n\\]\n\nhence \\(S_j\\) covers exactly \\(n-j\\) points.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{j+1\\mid 0\\le j\\le k-1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of ordinates that have **not** appeared as the second coordinate of any point on\nthe sunny lines.  The set \\(B\\) has cardinality \\(n-k\\).\n\nFor each \\(b\\in B\\) introduce the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_b:\\;y=b .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non\u2011sunny.  The total collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\}\\cup\\{H_b\\mid b\\in B\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) either lies on one of the sunny lines (if its ordinate belongs to\n\\(\\{j+1\\}\\)) or on the appropriate horizontal line \\(H_b\\); hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies the\nrequired covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\n\\begin{cases}\nx=1, & i=1,\\\\[4pt]\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{i-1}\\,x+\\dfrac{n+1}{i-1}, & i\\ge 2 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(i\\ge 2\\) the slope \\(-\\dfrac{1}{i-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently\n\\(L_i\\) is sunny.  The line \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\n(i,1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(1,i),\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Hence each point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) is contained in\n\\(L_a\\) (if \\(b=1\\)) or in \\(L_b\\) (if \\(a=1\\)); for points with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) we have\n\\((a,b)\\in L_a\\) because substituting \\(x=a\\) in the equation of \\(L_a\\) yields \\(y=b\\).\nThus the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) together cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and all\nof them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince we have exhibited a feasible configuration for every admissible value of \\(k\\) and have\nproved that \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible, the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. \u220e"
