nt,\n   so the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can supply at most \\(n-1\\) points to that row.\n   The row \\(y=1\\) (or, if \\(b_0=1\\), the row \\(y=2\\)) contains exactly \\(n\\) points of\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); therefore at least one of them would remain uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n   Hence a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line (i.e. with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines)\n   cannot exist.\n\n4. **Explicit constructions for all admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  All are non\u2011sunny and together they\n   cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *\\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \n   Take the sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\;y=x+j\\qquad(j=0,1,\\dots ,k-1),\n   \\]\n\n   and for each ordinate that does **not** appear among the numbers\n   \\(j+1\\;(0\\le j\\le k-1)\\) draw the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\}\\cup\\{y=b\\mid b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{j+1\\}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\n   and it covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  \n   For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{n+1-i}{i+1}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n   Substituting \\(x=i\\) into (2) gives \\(y=n+1-i\\), so the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on\n   \\(L_i\\).  Moreover, for any integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le i\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{i}{i+1}a + \\frac{n+1-i}{i+1}\n        = \\frac{ia + n+1-i}{i+1},\n   \\]\n\n   and the numerator is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n+1-a\\); consequently the point\n   \\((a,y)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and lies on \\(L_i\\).  Thus each line \\(L_i\\) contains\n   exactly \\(i\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_