-1\\); no vertical line is needed.\n\nSince a construction exists for every \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), these are precisely the admissible values.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering by vertical lines)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, non\u2011sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\supseteq S_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(S_n\\) with abscissa \\(i\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\},\n\\]\n\nhence they lie on \\(L_i\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Diagonal covering)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nDefine the following two families of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{(non\u2011sunny)}\\qquad &L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n-k),\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{(sunny)}\\qquad &M_j:\\;x-y=c_j,\\qquad \nc_j=n-2k+j\\;(1\\le j\\le k).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThen the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{\\,n-k}\\cup\\{M_j\\}_{j=1}^{\\,k}\\) are distinct,\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{\\,n-k}L_i\\;\\cup\\;\\bigcup_{j=1}^{\\,k}M_j\\;\\supseteq\\;S_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe lines \\(L_i\\) are vertical, hence non\u2011sunny; the lines \\(M_j\\) have slope\n\\(1\\), therefore they are sunny (they are not parallel to the prohibited\ndirections).  Distinctness is obvious because the \\(L_i\\) have different\n\\(x\\)-intercepts and the \\(M_j\\) have different constants \\(c_j\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\).\n\n*If \\(a>n-k\\)*, then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a-b\\ge a-(n+1-a)=2a-n-1\\ge\n2(n-k+1)-n-1=n-2k+1 .\n\\]\n\nAlso \\(b\\ge1\\) gives \\(a-b\\le a-1\\le n-1\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nn-2k+1\\le a-b\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe integer \\(a-b\\) therefore equals one of the \\(k\\) numbers  \n\n\\[\nc_1=n-2k+1,\\;c_2=n-2k+2,\\dots ,c_k=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the unique line \\(M_j\\) with\n\\(c_j=a-b\\).  Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by one of the\n\\(n\\) constructed lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (All values of \\(k\\) are attainable)\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines\nis exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Existence.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) Lemma\u202f2 provides a family of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.  Hence every such \\(k\\) occurs.\n\n*Necessity.*  \nObviously the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines,\nso \\(k\\le n\\); also \\(k\\) is a non\u2011negative integer.  Therefore the only\npossible values are those listed above.\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Remark on the pigeonhole principle  \n\nIn the proof of Lemma\u202f2 we used the pigeonhole principle in the following\nform: the \\(k\\) integers  \n\n\\[\nc_1,c_2,\\dots ,c_k=n-2k+1,\\dots ,n-1\n\\]\n\nform a complete set of \u201choles\u2019\u2019 for the quantity \\(a-b\\) of any point\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a>n-k\\).  Since each such point yields an integer\n\\(a-b\\) lying between the smallest and the largest hole, it must fall into\none of the holes, i.e. onto one of the sunny lines \\(M_j\\).  This guarantees\nthat the \\(k\\) sunny lines indeed cover all remaining points.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a family\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are precisely the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).  The constructions\nabove are explicit and rely only on elementary counting together with the\npigeonhole principle."
