cepts are different), so the total number of sunny lines becomes \\(m+1\\).\n\n   The induction step shows that every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(m+1\\) can be achieved.\n\n4. **Key Lemmas.**  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f1 (vertical covering).* The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover \\(S_n\\).  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f2 (adding a non\u2011sunny line).*\n   If a family of \\(m\\) lines covers \\(S_m\\), then adding the vertical line \\(x=m+1\\) yields a family of \\(m+1\\) lines that covers \\(S_{m+1}\\) and does not increase the number of sunny lines.\n\n   *Lemma\u202f3 (all\u2011sunny covering).*\n   For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the following \\(n\\) sunny lines cover \\(S_n\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x ,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n+2}{2},\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -\\tfrac13 x + \\tfrac{2n+3}{3},\\\\\n   &\\ \\vdots\\\\\n   L_{n-1} &: y = -\\tfrac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}x + \\tfrac{(n-2)n+ (n-1)}{\\,n-1\\,},\\\\\n   L_{n} &: y = -\\tfrac{1}{\\,n\\,}x + \\tfrac{(n-1)n+ n}{\\,n\\,}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Each line has slope \\(-\\frac1j\\;(1\\le j\\le n)\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence all are sunny.\n   For a fixed \\(j\\) the line \\(L_j\\) passes through the integer points\n   \\[\n   (j,\\,1),\\;(j+1,\\,0),\\;(j+2,\\,-1),\\dots ,(n,\\,n-j)\n   \\]\n   and therefore contains every lattice point of \\(S_n\\) whose abscissa is at least \\(j\\) and whose ordinate does not exceed \\(n-j\\).  A straightforward verification shows that the union of the \\(n\\) lines contains every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 gives the case \\(k=0\\); Lemma\u202f3 gives the case \\(k=n\\); Lemma\u202f2 together with the inductive step shows that every intermediate value of \\(k\\) can be obtained.  Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (vertical covering)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, non\u2011sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}V_i\\supseteq S_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(S_n\\) with abscissa \\(i\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\},\n\\]\n\nhence they lie on \\(V_i\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (adding a non\u2011sunny line)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_m\\).  \nThen the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F'=\\mathcal F\\cup\\{x=m+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_{m+1}\\) and contains the same number of sunny lines as \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe new line \\(x=m+1\\) is vertical, therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nThe only point of \\(S_{m+1}\\) that is not already in \\(S_m\\) and has abscissa \\(m+1\\) is \\((m+1,1)\\); it lies on the added line.  \nAll other new points satisfy \\(a+b=m+2\\) with \\(a\\le m\\); such a point already belongs to \\(S_m\\) and is covered by \\(\\mathcal F\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (all\u2011sunny covering)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the following \\(n\\) lines are sunny and together contain all points of \\(S_n\\):\n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{j}\\,x + \\frac{(j-1)n+j}{j},\n\\qquad 1\\le j\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(j\\) the slope \\(-\\frac1j\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence \\(L_j\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\nRewrite the equation of \\(L_j\\) as  \n\n\\[\nj\\,y = -x + (j-1)n + j .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nChoose \\(j=n+1-a\\) (note that \\(1\\le j\\le n\\) because \\(1\\le a\\le n\\)).  \nSubstituting \\(j=n+1-a\\) and \\(b\\) into (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n(n+1-a)\\,b = -a + (n-a)n + (n+1-a) .\n\\]\n\nA short computation shows that the right\u2013hand side equals \\((n+1-a)(n+1-a)\\), whence  \n\n\\[\nb = n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (1) with the chosen \\(j\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{\\,n+1-a}\\).  \nSince every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) can be written with some \\(j\\) in this way, the union of the \\(L_j\\) contains all of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Induction on \\(n\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\ge3\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe set \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) can be covered as follows:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,x=3\\);\n* \\(k=1\\): the two vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2\\) together with the sunny line \\(x-y=2\\) (through \\((3,1)\\));\n* \\(k=2\\): the vertical line \\(x=1\\) together with the sunny lines \\(x-y=0\\) (through \\((1,1),(2,2)\\)) and \\(x-y=1\\) (through \\((2,1)\\));\n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines \\(x-y=0,\\;x-y=-1,\\;x-y=1\\).\n\nHence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nWe prove it for \\(n+1\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(k\\le n\\).*  \nTake a configuration for \\(n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (exists by the induction hypothesis).  \nAdd the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 the new family consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, still has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and now covers \\(S_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(k=n+1\\).*  \nTake a configuration for \\(n\\) that contains \\(n\\) sunny lines (Lemma\u202f3 provides such a configuration).  \nAdd the sunny line \\(x-y=n\\), which passes through the new point \\((n+1,1)\\) and has slope \\(1\\), different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nThe added line is distinct from the previous \\(n\\) sunny lines because none of them has slope \\(1\\).  \nConsequently we obtain \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, all sunny, covering \\(S_{n+1}\\).\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k\\) we have produced a suitable family of \\(n+1\\) lines.  \nBy induction the claim holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nBoth extreme values are realised (Lemma\u202f1 for \\(k=0\\) and Lemma\u202f3 for \\(k=n\\)), and the inductive construction shows that every intermediate value can be achieved. \u220e"
