----------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  A bound for a sunny line\n\nWrite a sunny line as  \n\n\\[\npx+qy=c,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\},\\;p\\neq -q,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  \nFor \\((x,y)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le x\\le n,\\;1\\le y\\le n\\) and\n\\(x+y\\le n+1\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\npx+qy\\le |p|\\,x+|q|\\,y\\le\n\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\,(x+y)\\le\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\,(n+1).\n\\]\n\nSimilarly  \n\n\\[\npx+qy\\ge -\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\,(n+1).\n\\]\n\nThus the integer values taken by the left\u2011hand side on \\(S_n\\) lie in an\ninterval of length at most  \n\n\\[\n2\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nBecause consecutive integer values correspond to distinct points on the line,\nthe number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can belong to a single sunny line is at\nmost  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n(For even \\(n\\), \\(M=n/2\\); for odd \\(n\\), \\(M=(n+1)/2\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Necessary condition on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\), and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Let \\(t=n-k\\) be the number of\nnon\u2011sunny lines.  A non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\))\n intersects the triangle \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points, so it can\ncontribute at most \\(n\\) points to the covering.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le kM+tn = kM+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) from (5) and solve for \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kM+(n-k)n\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\bigl(n-M\\bigr)\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nUsing (7),\n\n\\[\nn-M=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac n2, & n\\ \\text{even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n-1}2, & n\\ \\text{odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nHence (9) yields\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1, & n\\ \\text{even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,   & n\\ \\text{odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSince obviously \\(k\\le n\\), (10) is the only restriction:  \n\n*if \\(n\\) is even, the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible; otherwise every\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\) satisfies the necessary inequality.*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Lemma \u2013 covering a triangle with sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\n\\forall m\\ge1\\;\\exists\\;m\\ \\text{distinct sunny lines covering }\nT_m:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(m=2r\\) (even) or \\(m=2r+1\\) (odd).\n\n*Even case* \\(m=2r\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x-(2i-1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,r),\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\\[\nL_{r+i}:\\; y = 2x-(2i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,r).\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes are \\(1\\) and \\(2\\); both are sunny.  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_{2r}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le2r+1\\).\n\n* If \\(a-b\\) is odd, write \\(a-b=2i-1\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le r\\); then\n\\((a,b)\\) satisfies (11) and lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\n* If \\(a-b\\) is even, write \\(a-2b=2i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le r\\); then\n\\((a,b)\\) satisfies (12) and lies on \\(L_{r+i}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{2r}\\) belongs to exactly one of the \\(2r\\) lines,\nwhich are distinct and sunny.\n\n*Odd case* \\(m=2r+1\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x-(2i-2)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,r+1),\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\\[\nL_{r+1+i}:\\; y = 2x-(2i-1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,r).\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nAgain the slopes are \\(1\\) and \\(2\\).  \nFor \\((a,b)\\in T_{2r+1}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le2r+2\\).\n\n* If \\(a-b\\) is even, write \\(a-b=2i-2\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le r+1\\); then\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\n* If \\(a-b\\) is odd, write \\(a-2b=2i-1\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le r\\); then\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{r+1+i}\\).\n\nHence all points of \\(T_{2r+1}\\) are covered by the \\(2r+1\\) distinct sunny\nlines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (10).  Put \\(t=n-k\\) (so \\(t\\ge0\\)).\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny part.**  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\n   \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct, non\u2011sunny, and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\)\n   whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).\n\n2. **Remaining triangle.**  \n   After deleting the columns \\(1,\\dots ,t\\) the uncovered points are\n\n   \\[\n   S_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{t}\\{x=i\\}\n   =\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge1,\\ a'+b\\le k+1\\}=T_k .\n   \\]\n\n   By the Lemma there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering exactly\n   \\(T_k\\).  Call them \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n3. **Whole configuration.**  \n   The set of lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=t\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(t+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\n   and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) obeying (10) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Conclusion\n\nCombining the necessary condition (10) with the explicit construction above,\nwe obtain the complete description of the possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\};\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nAll these values are attainable, and no other value of \\(k\\) can occur. \u220e"
