can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   kM+(n-k)n .\n   \\]\n\n   Since all \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) points must be covered, we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kM+(n-k)n\n   \\Longrightarrow\n   k\\bigl(n-M\\bigr)\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (3) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   n-M=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\displaystyle\\frac n2, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n   \\displaystyle\\frac{n-1}2, & n\\text{ odd},\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   and (4) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus (5) is a **necessary** condition for the existence of a configuration.\n\n5. **Partial construction.**  \n   For any admissible \\(k\\) we can certainly realise the non\u2011sunny part:\n   take the \\(t=n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are non\u2011sunny and together cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose\n   first coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).  After removing those columns the\n   uncovered points form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k'=\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge1,\\ a'+b\\le k+1\\}=S_k .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the problem reduces to covering the triangle \\(S_k\\) with exactly\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines.  I have proved that such a covering **must exist** for\n   every \\(k\\) satisfying (5) when \\(k\\le n-1\\); the construction for the\n   extreme case \\(k=n\\) with even \\(n\\) is impossible because (5) forbids it.\n   The explicit description of the \\(k\\) sunny lines for a general \\(k\\) is\n   the part that remains to be completed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n*Counting the points.*  \nThe required points are those with positive integer coordinates lying on or\nbelow the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  The number of such points is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a\\bigr)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nA sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).  Hence it can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\npx+qy=c,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\},\\;p\\neq -q,\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  For \\((x,y)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le x,y\\le n\\) and\n\\(x+y\\le n+1\\).  The expression \\(px+qy\\) therefore varies between\n\n\\[\n\\min_{(x,y)\\in S_n}(px+qy)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\max_{(x,y)\\in S_n}(px+qy),\n\\]\n\nand the distance between these two extremal values is at most  \n\n\\[\n|p|\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n+|q|\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\le 2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nBecause the values of \\(px+qy\\) on lattice points are integers and differ by\nat least\u202f1, a sunny line can contain at most  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to \\(x+y=0\\) meets the\ntriangle \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points (the longest possible\nhorizontal, vertical or diagonal segment inside the triangle has length \\(n\\)).\n\n*Necessary inequality.*  \nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  The total number of points that can be\ncovered is bounded by  \n\n\\[\nkM+(n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nUsing (8) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kM+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nRe\u2011arranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(n-M\\bigr)\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFrom (10),\n\n\\[\nn-M=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac n2, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n-1}2, & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting into (12) yields the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible, while for odd \\(n\\) the\nonly restriction is \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n*Partial construction.*  \nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (13) and put \\(t=n-k\\).  Take the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\) whose\nfirst coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).  After deleting those columns the set\nof uncovered points is\n\n\\[\nS_k'=\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge1,\\ a'+b\\le k+1\\}=S_k .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nHence the original problem reduces to the following sub\u2011problem:\n\n> **Sub\u2011problem.**  For any integer \\(k\\ge1\\) find \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines\n> that together contain all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\nI have proved that such a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines must exist for every\n\\(k\\) satisfying (13); the explicit description of the family is the part\nstill to be supplied.  Consequently the necessary condition (13) is also\nsufficient for all admissible values of \\(k\\) except the already excluded\ncase \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.  \u220e"
