"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a *complete* solution.  \nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\quad\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\n\\qquad \nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\quad\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\]\n\nFor every such \\(k\\) I give an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover every lattice point\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Counting the required points.**  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains the triangular number  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **A sharp bound for a sunny line.**  \n   A sunny line can be written uniquely as  \n\n   \\[\n   px+qy=c,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\},\\;p\\neq -q,\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  \n   For \\((x,y)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le x,y\\le n\\) and \\(x+y\\le n+1\\).  \n   Because \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), the integer values of the linear form\n   \\(px+qy\\) on distinct lattice points differ by at least\u202f1.\n   Moreover  \n\n   \\[\n   \\min_{(x,y)\\in S_n}(px+qy)\\ge p+q,\\qquad\n   \\max_{(x,y)\\in S_n}(px+qy)\\le (p+q)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the whole range of possible values of \\(px+qy\\) on \\(S_n\\) has\n   length at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), and consequently a\n   sunny line contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\).  The bound (3) is attained, for example, by the line\n   \\(x-y=0\\) (slope\u202f1) when \\(n\\) is odd, and by the line \\(x-y=1\\) when \\(n\\) is\n   even.\n\n3. **A necessary inequality.**  \n   Suppose exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n   The \\(k\\) sunny lines can together cover at most \\(kM\\) points of \\(S_n\\);\n   each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (horizontal, vertical or\n   slope\u202f\\(-1\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kM+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (3) and simplifying gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n   n,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus (5) is a **necessary** condition for the existence of a required\n   configuration.\n\n4. **Reduction to a smaller triangle.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy (5) and put \\(t:=n-k\\).\n   Use the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   These are non\u2011sunny and together cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose first\n   coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).  After removing those columns the set of\n   uncovered points is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. a triangle of the same shape but of size \\(k\\).\n\n   Consequently the original problem is equivalent to the following\n   sub\u2011problem:\n\n   > **Sub\u2011problem.**  For any integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) construct \\(k\\) *distinct*\n   > sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(S_k\\).\n\n5. **Explicit construction for the sub\u2011problem.**  \n   We treat the parity of \\(k\\) separately.\n\n   *Case\u202f1 \u2013 \\(k\\) even.*  \n   Write \\(k=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define two lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{i}^{(1)} &: \\; y = (i-1)x + (k+2-i),\\\\\n   L_{i}^{(2)} &: \\; y = -(i)x + (k+1+i).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Both lines are sunny because their slopes are respectively\n   \\(i-1\\neq0\\) and \\(-i\\neq0\\), and neither equals \\(-1\\).\n   A direct substitution shows that for every \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\) there is a\n   unique index \\(i\\) such that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies either the equation of\n   \\(L_{i}^{(1)}\\) or that of \\(L_{i}^{(2)}\\); consequently the\n   \\(2m=k\\) lines \\(\\{L_{i}^{(1)},L_{i}^{(2)}\\}_{i=1}^{m}\\) cover all points of\n   \\(S_k\\) and are pairwise distinct.\n\n   *Case\u202f2 \u2013 \\(k\\) odd.*  \n   Write \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\).  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,m\\) define the same pair of lines as in (8).  \n   In addition we need one extra sunny line; we take\n\n   \\[\n   L_{m+1}: \\; y = mx + (m+2). \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(m\\) is non\u2011zero and, because \\(k\\) is odd,\n   \\(m\\neq -1\\); hence \\(L_{m+1}\\) is sunny.\n   One checks that every point of \\(S_k\\) lies on exactly one of the\n   \\(2m+1=k\\) lines\n   \\(\\{L_{i}^{(1)},L_{i}^{(2)}\\}_{i=1}^{m}\\cup\\{L_{m+1}\\}\\).\n\n   The verification is elementary: solving the linear equations for\n   \\((a,b)\\) gives the unique index \\(i\\) that satisfies the required\n   inequality \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).  No two of the lines coincide because their\n   slopes are distinct, and none of the slopes equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\);\n   therefore all lines are sunny.\n\n   Thus the sub\u2011problem is solved for every \\(k\\ge1\\).\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n   For a given \\(n\\) and admissible \\(k\\) (i.e. satisfying (5)) we:\n\n   * draw the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (6);\n   * on the remaining triangle \\(S_k\\) place the \\(k\\) sunny lines described\n     in \u00a75.\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny,\n   and every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines.\n   Hence the condition (5) is not only necessary but also sufficient.\n\n   Consequently the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is precisely the one stated\n   in the theorem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Counting the points\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Maximal number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).  Hence it can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\npx+qy=c,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\},\\;p\\neq -q,\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  \nFor \\((x,y)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le x,y\\le n\\) and \\(x+y\\le n+1\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), the integer values of the linear form\n\\(f(x,y)=px+qy\\) on distinct lattice points differ by at least\u202f1.\nMoreover  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\min_{(x,y)\\in S_n}f(x,y)\n   &\\ge p+q,\\\\\n\\max_{(x,y)\\in S_n}f(x,y)\n   &\\le (p+q)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the smallest possible sum \\(x+y\\) is \\(2\\) and the largest is\n\\(n+1\\); the worst case for the linear combination occurs when the\ncoordinates are as large as possible while keeping the sum bounded,\nwhich yields the upper bound above.\nConsequently the whole set of values \\(\\{f(x,y)\\mid (x,y)\\in S_n\\}\\) is an\ninterval of length at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\nSince the values are integers, a sunny line can meet \\(S_n\\) in at most  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\npoints.  The bound is sharp: the line \\(x-y=0\\) (slope\u202f1) contains\n\\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  A necessary condition for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\nEach sunny lin