 sunny.\nEach sunny line contributes at most \\(M\\) points, while each non\u2011sunny line\n(horizontal, vertical or slope\u202f\\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of\n\\(S_n\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kM+(n-k)n .\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nInsert (12).  If \\(n\\) is even, \\(M=n/2\\); if \\(n\\) is odd,\n\\(M=(n+1)/2\\).  After elementary algebra we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThus (14) is a **necessary** restriction on the possible number of sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Reduction to a triangle of size \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (14) and set \\(t:=n-k\\).\nConsider the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\) whose\nfirst coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).  Removing those columns leaves exactly\n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nthe same triangular set but of size \\(k\\).  Consequently the original\nproblem is equivalent to the following:\n\n> **Sub\u2011problem.**  For any integer \\(k\\ge1\\) construct \\(k\\) distinct sunny\n> lines whose union contains all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Construction for the sub\u2011problem\n\nWe treat the parity of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5.1  Even \\(k\\)\n\nWrite \\(k=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define the two lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{i}^{(1)} &: \\; y = (i-1)x + (k+2-i),\\\\\nL_{i}^{(2)} &: \\; y = -i\\,x + (k+1+i).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n*Both slopes are non\u2011zero and different from \\(-1\\); therefore the lines are\nsunny.*  \nWe claim that the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\{L_{i}^{(1)},L_{i}^{(2)}\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_k\\).\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le k+1=2m+1\\), exactly one\nof the two inequalities  \n\n\\[\nb\\le (i-1)a + (k+2-i),\\qquad\nb\\ge -i\\,a + (k+1+i)\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nholds for a unique index \\(i\\) (the left\u2011hand side of (19) is increasing in\n\\(i\\), the right\u2011hand side is decreasing).  Solving the corresponding\nequality shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies either the equation of\n\\(L_{i}^{(1)}\\) or that of \\(L_{i}^{(2)}\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on a line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThe slopes in (17) are pairwise distinct, so the lines are distinct.\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of exactly \\(2m=k\\) sunny lines covering all\npoints of \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5.2  Odd \\(k\\)\n\nWrite \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\).\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,m\\) we again use the pair of lines \\(L_{i}^{(1)},L_{i}^{(2)}\\)\ndefined in (17).  In addition we need one more sunny line; we take  \n\n\\[\nL_{m+1}: \\; y = m\\,x + (m+2). \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(m\\) is non\u2011zero; because \\(k\\) is odd we have \\(m\\neq -1\\), so\n\\(L_{m+1}\\) is sunny.  The set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\{L_{i}^{(1)},L_{i}^{(2)}\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}\\cup\\{L_{m+1}\\}\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(2m+1=k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nThe same argument as in the even case shows that every \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\)\nsatisfies either the equation of some \\(L_{i}^{(1)}\\) or of some\n\\(L_{i}^{(2)}\\) (for a suitable \\(i\\le m\\)), or, when \\(a\\ge m+1\\), the\nequation of \\(L_{m+1}\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers all points of\n\\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) be given and let \\(k\\) satisfy (14).\n\n* If \\(k=0\\) we simply take the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines\n  \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered.\n\n* If \\(k\\ge1\\) we proceed as follows.\n\n  1. Draw the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (15).  They are non\u2011sunny and cover\n     all points of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\).\n\n  2. On the remaining triangle \\(S_k\\) place the \\(k\\) sunny lines given by\n     the construction of \u00a72.5 (according to the parity of \\(k\\)).\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and by\nconstruction every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\nTherefore a configuration exists for every \\(k\\) satisfying (14).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Final answer\n\nCombining the necessary condition (14) with the explicit construction,\nthe set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\quad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\\n&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\quad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(k\\) the procedure described above yields \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all lattice points\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \u220e"
