ibed\n     in \u00a75.\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny,\n   and by construction every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the\n   lines.  Hence condition (2.5) is not only necessary but also sufficient.\n\n   Consequently the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely the\n   one stated in the theorem.  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Number of required points\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Maximum number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it can be written as  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+c,\\qquad m\\neq0,\\;m\\neq-1 .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lie on such a line.  The conditions\n\\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\) become, after substituting\n\\(b=ma+c\\),\n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n,\\qquad\n1\\le ma+c\\le n+1-a .\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.3) we obtain the interval of admissible integer abscissas.\nIf \\(m>0\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-c}{m}\\le a\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{1+m},\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nand if \\(m<0\\) the inequalities are reversed; in both cases the length of\nthe interval is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+1}{|1+m|}. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nBecause a sunny line is not parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\), we have\n\\(|1+m|\\ge 2\\).  Hence the interval (2.4)\u2013(2.5) contains at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) integers.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any sunny line meets at most }M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\text{ points of }S_n.}\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  By (2.6) the sunny lines together cover at\nmost \\(kM\\) points.  Each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines is parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); such a line can contain at\nmost \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kM+(n-k)n .\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(M\\) from (2.6) and simplifying gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nThus (2.8) is a **necessary** condition for the existence of a required\nconfiguration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Reduction to a smaller triangle\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (2.8) and put \\(t:=n-k\\).\nDraw the \\(t\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not\nexceed \\(t\\).  After removing those columns the uncovered points are exactly\n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\ni.e. a triangle of the same shape but of size \\(k\\).\n\nHence it remains to prove the following sub\u2011problem.\n\n> **Sub\u2011problem.** For any integer \\(k\\ge1\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny\n> lines whose union contains all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Solution of the sub\u2011problem\n\nWe treat the parity of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5.1  Even \\(k\\)\n\nWrite \\(k=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define the two lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i^{(1)} &: \\; y = (i-1)x + (k+2-i),\\\\[2mm]\nL_i^{(2)} &: \\; y = -i\\,x + (k+1+i).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes are non\u2011zero and different from \\(-1\\); consequently all the\n\\(2m\\) lines are sunny and pairwise distinct (their slopes are distinct).\n\n**Claim.** The family \\(\\mathcal L_k:=\\{L_i^{(1)},L_i^{(2)}\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}\\)\ncovers \\(S_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le 2m+1\\) we have either \\(a\\le m\\) or\n\\(b\\le m\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le m\\).  Put \\(i:=a\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le m\\)).  Then\n\n\\[\n(i-1)a + (k+2-i)= (i-1)i + (2m+2-i)= i + (2m+1-i)=k+1-i .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we obtain \\(b = (i-1)a + (k+2-i)\\); thus\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i^{(1)}\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(b\\le m\\).  Put \\(i:=b\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le m\\)).  Then\n\n\\[\n-i\\,b + (k+1+i)= -i i + (2m+1+i)= (2m+1)-i = k+1-i .\n\\]\n\nAgain using \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we get \\(a = -i\\,b + (k+1+i)\\); hence\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i^{(2)}\\).\n\nIn either case \\((a,b)\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\); the claim is\nproved. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5.2  Odd \\(k\\)\n\nWrite \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\).\nTake the \\(2m\\) lines given by (2.11) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,m\\) and add the\nextra sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{m+1}: \\; y = m\\,x + (m+2). \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(m\\) is non\u2011zero; because \\(k\\) is odd we have \\(m\\neq-1\\), so the\nline is sunny and distinct from all lines in (2.11).\n\n**Claim.** The family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\{L_i^{(1)},L_i^{(2)}\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}\\cup\\{L_{m+1}\\}\n\\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\).  As before either \\(a\\le m\\) or \\(b\\le m\\) or\n\\((a,b)=(m+1,m+1)\\) (the only point not covered by the first two\nalternatives).\n\nIf \\(a\\le m\\) we argue exactly as in the even case and obtain that\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a^{(1)}\\); if \\(b\\le m\\) we obtain that it