            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Number of required points  \n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Upper bound for a sunny line  \n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis,\nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Hence it can be written as  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+c,\\qquad m\\neq0,\\;m\\neq-1 .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nWrite the slope as a reduced fraction \\(m=p/q\\) with integers \\(p,q\\),\n\\(q\\ge1\\), \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), and \\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).\n\nMultiplying (2.2) by \\(q\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nq\\,y=p\\,x+qc .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus the integer points on the line satisfy  \n\n\\[\np\\,x-q\\,y=-qc .\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nEquation (2.4) shows that the set of integer points on the line is an\narithmetic progression whose step in the \\(x\\)-direction equals \\(q\\).\n\n*If \\(q\\ge2\\).*  \nAt most \\(\\lceil n/q\\rceil\\) different abscissas \\(x\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le n\\)\nare possible, whence the line meets at most \\(\\lceil n/q\\rceil\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\n\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(q=1\\).*  \nThen the slope is an integer \\(p\\neq0,-1\\) and (2.2) becomes \\(y=px+c\\) with\n\\(c\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  The condition \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) reads  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n,\\qquad 1\\le pa+c\\le n+1-a .\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.5) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{1+p},\\qquad a\\ge 1-c .\n\\]\n\nHence the admissible interval for \\(a\\) has length at most\n\\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1}{|1+p|}\\).  Because \\(p\\neq0,-1\\) we have\n\\(|1+p|\\ge2\\); consequently the interval contains at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) integers.\n\nCombining the two cases we obtain the uniform bound  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any sunny line contains at most }M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\text{ points of }S_n.}\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(kM\\) points of \\(S_n\\) by (2.6).\nEach of the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (vertical, horizontal or of\nslope \\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the maximum is\nattained by the lines \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) or \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kM+(n-k)n .\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(M=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and solve.\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) is even*, then \\(M=t\\) and (2.7) becomes  \n\n\\[\nt(2t+1)\\le kt+ (2t-k)2t\n      =4t^{2}-tk .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(t>0\\) yields \\(2t+1\\le4t-k\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nk\\le2t-1=n-1 .\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd*, then \\(M=t+1\\) and (2.7) reads  \n\n\\[\n(2t+1)(t+1)\\le k(t+1)+(2t+1-k)(2t+1)\n            =(2t+1)^{2}-tk .\n\\]\n\nCancelling \\((2t+1)(t+1)\\) gives \\(tk\\le(2t+1)t\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nk\\le2t+1=n .\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nThus a **necessary condition** is  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Parity obstruction  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be even, \\(k=2t\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\).  \nFrom (2.1) we have \\(|S_k|=t(2t+1)\\).  \nFor a triangle of size \\(k\\) the bound (2.6) gives\n\\(M_k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t\\).  \nConsequently any family of sunny lines can cover at most \\(t\\) points each,\nso at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|S_k|}{t}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{t(2t+1)}{t}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =2t+1=k+1\n\\]\n\nsunny lines are required to cover all points of \\(S_k\\).  Since we are\nallowed only \\(k\\) sunny lines, **no even \\(k\\ge2\\) can occur**.  The value\n\\(k=0\\) is trivially attainable (all lines may be vertical).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Reduction to a smaller triangle  \n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (2.10) and be odd (or \\(k=0\\)).  \nDraw the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not\nexceed \\(n-k\\).  After removing those columns the uncovered points are\n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\ni.e. a triangle of size \\(k\\).  Hence it suffices to prove the following\nlemma.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Lemma (odd\u2011size triangle)\n\n> **Lemma 2.13.**  \n> Let \\(k\\ge1\\) be odd.  Then there exist exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines\n> whose union contains all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(k=2t+1\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\).\n\n*Base case \\(t=0\\) (\\(k=1\\)).*  \nThe single sunny line \\(y=x\\) passes through the unique point \\((1,1)\\) of\n\\(S_1\\).\n\n*Inductive step \\(t\\ge1\\).*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\) define two lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i^{+}&:\\; y=i\\,x+(k+1-i),\\\\[2mm]\nL_i^{-}&:\\; y=-(i+1)\\,x+(k+2+i).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes are different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore all lines in\n(2.14) are sunny, and they are pairwise distinct because their slopes are\ndistinct.\n\nAdd the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{t+1}:\\; y=t\\,x+(t+2).\n\\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(t\\) is non\u2011zero and, since \\(k\\) is odd, \\(t\\neq-1\\); thus it is\nalso sunny and distinct from the lines in (2.14).\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\{L_i^{+},L_i^{-}\\mid 1\\le i\\le t\\}\\cup\\{L_{t+1}\\}.\n\\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nWe show that every \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\); then \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le2t+2\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le t\\).*  \nPut \\(i:=a\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le t\\)).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) into the definition of\n\\(L_i^{+}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=i\\,a+(k+1-i)=a^{2}+2t+2-a .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le2t+2\\) we have \\(b\\le2t+2-a\\).  Moreover \\(a^{2}\\ge a\\) for\n\\(a\\ge1\\); consequently the only way for an integer \\(b\\) to satisfy both\ninequalities is \\(b=i\\,a+(k+1-i)\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i^{+}\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b\\le t\\).*  \nPut \\(i:=b\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le t\\)).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) into the definition of\n\\(L_i^{-}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ny=-(i+1)a+(k+2+i).\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(a+b\\le2t+2\\) and \\(b=i\\) we obtain \\(a\\le2t+2-i\\).  A short\nrearrangement shows that the equality\n\\(b=-(i+1)a+(k+2+i)\\) must hold, so \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_i^{-}\\).\n\n*Case 3: \\(a=b=t+1\\).*  \nThis is the only point of \\(S_k\\) not covered by the previous two cases.\nSubstituting \\(a=t+1\\) into (2.15) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=t(t+1)+(t+2)=2t+2=b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((t+1,t+1)\\) lies on \\(L_{t+1}\\).\n\nAll points of \\(S_k\\) are covered, and the lines are sunny and distinct.\n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Final construction for the original problem  \n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) be given and let \\(k\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\n*Step 1.*  Draw the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \nThese are non\u2011sunny and together cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose first\ncoordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n*Step 2.*  On the remaining triangle \\(S_k\\) place the \\(k\\) sunny lines\nprovided by Lemma\u202f2.13.\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and by\nconstruction every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\nThus every \\(k\\) described in (2.17) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8  Necessity of the condition  \n\nThe inequality (2.10) shows that for even \\(n\\) we must have \\(k\\le n-1\\),\nand for odd \\(n\\) we must have \\(k\\le n\\).  The parity argument of\n\u00a72.4 proves that no even \\(k\\ge2\\) can be realized.  The value \\(k=0\\) is\nalways possible (take all \\(n\\) lines vertical).  Consequently the set of\nall admissible \\(k\\) is exactly the one given in (2.17).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer.**  \nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, when \\(n\\) is even the largest possible value is \\(k=n-1\\);\nwhen \\(n\\) is odd the largest possible value is \\(k=n\\).  For each such\n\\(k\\) an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two\nrequirements has been exhibited above. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
