            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Number of required points  \n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Upper bound for a sunny line  \n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis,\nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Hence it can be written as  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+c,\\qquad m\\neq0,\\;m\\neq-1 .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nWrite the slope as a reduced fraction \\(m=p/q\\) with integers \\(p,q\\),\n\\(q\\ge1\\), \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), and \\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).\nMultiplying (2.2) by \\(q\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nq\\,y=p\\,x+qc .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus an integer point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line iff  \n\n\\[\np\\,a-q\\,b=-qc .\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nEquation (2.4) shows that the set of integer points on the line is an\narithmetic progression whose step in the \\(x\\)-direction equals \\(q\\).\n\n*If \\(q\\ge2\\).*  \nAt most \\(\\lceil n/q\\rceil\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) different\nabscissas \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) can occur, whence the line meets at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(q=1\\).*  \nThen the slope is an integer \\(p\\neq0,-1\\) and (2.2) becomes \\(y=px+c\\) with\n\\(c\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  The condition \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) reads  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n,\\qquad 1\\le pa+c\\le n+1-a .\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.5) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{1+p},\\qquad a\\ge 1-c .\n\\]\n\nHence the admissible interval for \\(a\\) has length at most\n\\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1}{|1+p|}\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\); consequently the number\nof integer \\(a\\) (and therefore of points of \\(S_n\\)) on the line does not\nexceed \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n**Conclusion.** Every sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor}\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(kM\\) points of \\(S_n\\) by (2.6).\nEach of the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (vertical, horizontal or of\nslope \\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the maximum is attained\nby the lines \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) or \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kM+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(M=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and solve.\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) is even*, then \\(M=t\\) and (2.7) becomes  \n\n\\[\nt(2t+1)\\le kt+(2t-k)2t,\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to \\(k\\le 2t-1=n-1\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd*, then \\(M=t+1\\) and (2.7) yields  \n\n\\[\n(2t+1)(t+1)\\le k(t+1)+(2t+1-k)(2t+1),\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to \\(k\\le 2t+1=n\\).\n\nThus a **necessary condition** is  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Parity obstruction  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be even, \\(k=2t\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\).  \nFrom (2.6) applied to the triangle \\(S_k\\) we obtain\n\\(M_k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t\\).\nSince \\(|S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=t(2t+1)\\), covering \\(S_k\\) with only \\(k\\) sunny\nlines would require at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|S_k|}{M_k}\\Bigr\\rceil\n =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{t(2t+1)}{t}\\Bigr\\rceil\n =2t+1=k+1\n\\]\n\nlines, a contradiction.  Hence **no even \\(k\\ge2\\) can occur**.  The value\n\\(k=0\\) is trivially realizable (all lines may be vertical).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Reduction to a smaller triangle  \n\nAssume that \\(k\\) satisfies (2.8) and is odd (or \\(k=0\\)).\nDraw the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed\n\\(n-k\\).  After deleting those columns the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\ni.e. a triangle of the same shape but of size \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Lemma (covering an odd\u2011size triangle)\n\n> **Lemma 2.11.**  \n> Let \\(k\\ge1\\) be odd and write \\(k=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge0\\)).  \n> There exist exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all\n> points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Base case \\(t=0\\) (\\(k=1\\)).*  \nThe single sunny line \\(y=x\\) passes through the unique point \\((1,1)\\) of\n\\(S_1\\).\n\n*Inductive step \\(t\\ge1\\).*  \nDefine the following \\(2t+1\\) lines.\n\n*For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\)*\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i^{+}&:\\; y=i\\,x+(t+1-i),\\\\[2mm]\nL_i^{-}&:\\; y=-(i+1)\\,x+(2t+3-i).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\n*One additional line*\n\\[\nL_{0}:\\; y=x+t+1 .\n\\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes occurring in (2.12)\u2013(2.13) are different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\);\nhence every line is sunny, and the \\(2t+1\\) lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.6.1  Points on the lines\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le t\\)) the integer points of \\(L_i^{+}\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(a,\\;i a+(t+1-i)\\bigr)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,t+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\nFor each such point we have  \n\n\\[\na+\\bigl(i a+(t+1-i)\\bigr)=(i+1)a+(t+1-i)\\le (i+1)(t+1)+(t+1-i)=2t+2,\n\\]\n\nso the whole set (2.14) lies inside \\(S_k\\).  Conversely, if a point of\n\\(S_k\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i^{+}\\) then its abscissa must belong to\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,t+1\\}\\); therefore (2.14) is exactly the set of points of\n\\(S_k\\) lying on \\(L_i^{+}\\).  Hence \\(L_i^{+}\\) contains precisely \\(t+1\\)\npoints of \\(S_k\\).\n\nAnalogously, the integer points of \\(L_i^{-}\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(a,\\;-(i+1)a+(2t+3-i)\\bigr)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,t+1\\,\\bigr\\},\n\\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\nand a direct computation shows that each of them satisfies\n\\(a+b\\le2t+2\\); thus (2.15) is exactly the set of points of \\(S_k\\) on\n\\(L_i^{-}\\).  Consequently \\(L_i^{-}\\) also contains \\(t+1\\) points of\n\\(S_k\\).\n\nFor the line \\(L_{0}\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nL_{0}\\cap S_k=\\bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(a,\\;a+t+1\\bigr)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,t+1\\,\\bigr\\},\n\\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nwhich again consists of \\(t+1\\) points because\n\\(a+(a+t+1)=2a+t+1\\le2t+2\\) for \\(a\\le t+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.6.2  Disjointness\n\nThe three families (2.14), (2.15) and (2.16) are pairwise disjoint.\nIndeed, if a point satisfied both the equation of \\(L_i^{+}\\) and that of\n\\(L_j^{+}\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\), we would have\n\\(i a+(t+1-i)=j a+(t+1-j)\\), i.e. \\((i-j)a=j-i\\), which forces \\(a=1\\) and\n\\(i=j\\), a contradiction.  Similar elementary calculations exclude any\nintersection between the other two families.  Hence the families are\ndisjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.6.3  Covering all points of \\(S_k\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\); then \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le2t+2\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\ge t+1\\)*, write \\(b-a=t+1+i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le t-1\\).  \nThen \\(b=i a+(t+1-i)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{i+1}^{+}\\).\n\n*If \\(a-b\\ge t+1\\)*, write \\(a-b=t+1+i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le t-1\\).  \nThen \\(b=-(i+1)a+(2t+3-i)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{i+1}^{-}\\).\n\n*If \\(|a-b|\\le t\\)*, the inequality \\(a+b\\le2t+2\\) forces\n\\(b=a+t+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_{0}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_k\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines listed in\n(2.12)\u2013(2.13).  Since each of the \\(2t+1\\) lines contains \\(t+1\\) points,\nthe total number of covered points is  \n\n\\[\n(2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|S_k|,\n\\]\n\nso all points of \\(S_k\\) are covered.  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Final construction for the original problem  \n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\n* If \\(k=0\\), take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); none of them is\n  sunny and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of them.\n\n* If \\(k\\ge1\\) is odd, proceed as follows.\n\n  1. Draw the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n  2. On the remaining triangle \\(S_k\\) place the \\(k\\) sunny lines given by\n     Lemma\u202f2.11.\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and by\nconstruction every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8  Necessity of the condition  \n\nSections\u202f2.3 and\u202f2.4 show that any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy (2.8) and must\nbe either \\(0\\) or odd.  Combining this with the explicit construction of\nSection\u202f2.7 yields the complete description (2.17).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nBoth the necessity and the sufficiency have been proved rigorously. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
