ains precisely \\(t+1\\)\npoints of \\(S_k\\).\n\nAnalogously, the integer points of \\(L_i^{-}\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(a,\\;-(i+1)a+(2t+3-i)\\bigr)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,t+1\\,\\bigr\\},\n\\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\nand a direct computation shows that each of them satisfies\n\\(a+b\\le2t+2\\); thus (2.15) is exactly the set of points of \\(S_k\\) on\n\\(L_i^{-}\\).  Consequently \\(L_i^{-}\\) also contains \\(t+1\\) points of\n\\(S_k\\).\n\nFor the line \\(L_{0}\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nL_{0}\\cap S_k=\\bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(a,\\;a+t+1\\bigr)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,t+1\\,\\bigr\\},\n\\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nwhich again consists of \\(t+1\\) points because\n\\(a+(a+t+1)=2a+t+1\\le2t+2\\) for \\(a\\le t+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.6.2  Disjointness\n\nThe three families (2.14), (2.15) and (2.16) are pairwise disjoint.\nIndeed, if a point satisfied both the equation of \\(L_i^{+}\\) and that of\n\\(L_j^{+}\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\), we would have\n\\(i a+(t+1-i)=j a+(t+1-j)\\), i.e. \\((i-j)a=j-i\\), which forces \\(a=1\\) and\n\\(i=j\\), a contradiction.  Similar elementary calculations exclude any\nintersection between the other two families.  Hence the families are\ndisjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.6.3  Covering all points of \\(S_k\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\); then \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le2t+2\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\ge t+1\\)*, write \\(b-a=t+1+i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le t-1\\).  \nThen \\(b=i a+(t+1-i)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{i+1}^{+}\\).\n\n*If \\(a-b\\ge t+1\\)*, write \\(a-b=t+1+i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le t-1\\).  \nThen \\(b=-(i+1)a+(2t+3-i)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{i+1}^{-}\\).\n\n*If \\(|a-b|\\le t\\)*, the inequality \\(a+b\\le2t+2\\) forces\n\\(b=a+t+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_{0}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_k\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines listed in\n(2.12)\u2013(2.13).  Since each of the \\(2t