"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k\\text{ is odd and }1\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\{0\\}\\cup\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid k\\text{ odd},\\;k\\le n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Necessity** \u2013 we show that any configuration satisfying the two\n   requirements must have either no sunny line or an odd number of sunny\n   lines, and that the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).\n\n   *Lemma 1 (Parity).*  \n   Let a line be called *non\u2011sunny* if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\n   the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   In any admissible configuration the number of non\u2011sunny lines is\n   congruent to \\(n\\pmod 2\\). Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n   satisfies \\(k\\equiv 0\\) or \\(1\\pmod 2\\); i.e. \\(k=0\\) or \\(k\\) is odd.\n\n   *Proof idea.*  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\) of the triangular set\n   \\(\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) lies on exactly\n   three non\u2011sunny lines: \\(x=a\\), \\(y=b\\) and \\(x+y=a+b\\).  \n   Count the incidences between points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and the\n   non\u2011sunny lines in two ways.  Each point contributes three incidences,\n   giving a total of \\(3|\\mathcal T_n|=3\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  On the other hand,\n   a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains \\(n+1-c\\) points, a vertical line\n   \\(x=c\\) contains \\(n+1-c\\) points, and a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) contains\n   \\(c-1\\) points.  Summing these contributions over all non\u2011sunny lines\n   yields an expression that is congruent to the number of non\u2011sunny lines\n   modulo \\(2\\).  Equating the two counts forces the parity condition.\n\n   *Lemma 2 (Upper bound).*  \n   Because the total number of lines is \\(n\\), we obviously have\n   \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n2. **Sufficiency** \u2013 for every admissible \\(k\\) we construct an explicit\n   configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   The construction is by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n   *Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The set \\(\\mathcal T_3\\) consists of the three points\n   \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   - \\(k=0\\): use the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=2\\).  \n   - \\(k=1\\): keep the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\) and add a sunny\n     line through the remaining point \\((1,2)\\) (any line through that\n     point whose slope is neither \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) works).\n\n   Thus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Let \\(k\\) be admissible\n   for \\(n+2\\); i.e. either \\(k=0\\) or \\(k\\) is odd and \\(k\\le n+2\\).\n\n   - **Case \\(k=0\\).**  \n     Take the \\(n+2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n+2\\).  Every\n     point of \\(\\mathcal T_{\\,n+2}\\) has its first coordinate in\n     \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n+2\\}\\), hence is covered.  All lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n   - **Case \\(k\\ge 1\\) (odd).**  \n     Write \\(k=2t+1\\) with \\(t\\ge 0\\) and \\(2t+1\\le n+2\\).  \n     From the induction hypothesis we have a configuration\n     \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) for the triangle \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) that uses exactly\n     \\(2t-1\\) sunny lines (possible because \\(2t-1\\) is odd and\n     \\(\\le n\\)).  Extend \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) to a configuration for\n     \\(\\mathcal T_{\\,n+2}\\) as follows.\n\n     1. **Add two new rows and two new columns.**  \n        The points of \\(\\mathcal T_{\\,n+2}\\setminus\\mathcal T_n\\) are exactly\n        the points with either coordinate equal to \\(n+1\\) or \\(n+2\\) and\n        satisfying the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+3\\).  They form a border of\n        width two around \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n     2. **Cover the new border with three new non\u2011sunny lines**  \n        \\[\n        \\ell_1:\\;x=n+1,\\qquad\n        \\ell_2:\\;y=n+1,\\qquad\n        \\ell_3:\\;x+y=n+3 .\n        \\]\n        These three lines are distinct, non\u2011sunny and together contain\n        every newly added point.\n\n     3. **Introduce two new sunny lines**  \n        Choose the two points\n        \\[\n        P_1=(n+2,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,n+2)\n        \\]\n        (both belong to the new border).  Let \\(L_1\\) be the line through\n        \\(P_1\\) and \\((n,2)\\), and let \\(L_2\\) be the line through\n        \\(P_2\\) and \\((2,n)\\).  Both lines have slope \\(1\\) and \\(-1\\) respectively,\n        hence they are **not** parallel to any of the forbidden directions;\n        consequently they are sunny.  Moreover each of them covers exactly\n        one of the points \\(P_1,P_2\\) that are not already covered by the three\n        non\u2011sunny border lines.\n\n     4. **Count the lines.**  \n        The original configuration \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) contributed \\(n\\) lines,\n        of which \\(2t-1\\) were sunny.  We have added three non\u2011sunny lines\n        and two sunny lines, obtaining a total of\n        \\[\n        n+5\\;=\\;(n+2)+3\n        \\]\n        lines.  To keep the total number equal to \\(n+2\\) we delete any\n        three non\u2011sunny lines that are now redundant.  For instance we may\n        delete the vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,x=3\\) (they are still covered by\n        the remaining lines because the points they contained are either\n        already on a sunny line or on one of the three new border lines).\n        After this deletion we have exactly \\(n+2\\) lines, and the number of\n        sunny lines is\n        \\[\n        (2t-1)+2 = 2t+1 = k .\n        \\]\n\n     This completes the inductive construction for every odd \\(k\\le n+2\\).\n\n   By induction the construction works for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nCombining the necessity and sufficiency parts we obtain the claimed\ncharacterisation of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad\n|\\mathcal T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Necessity\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.1 (Parity)\n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering\ncondition for \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).  Denote by \\(s\\) the number of\nnon\u2011sunny lines in the configuration.  Then  \n\n\\[\ns\\equiv n\\pmod 2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) lies on exactly three non\u2011sunny\nlines: the vertical line \\(x=a\\), the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) and the\ndiagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\).  Hence the total number of incidences between\npoints of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and non\u2011sunny lines equals  \n\n\\[\nI=3|\\mathcal T_n|=3\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nNow count the same incidences by summing, for each non\u2011sunny line,\nthe number of points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) that lie on it.\n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n)\\) contains the points\n  \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); therefore it contributes\n  \\(n+1-c\\) incidences.\n\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n)\\) contributes likewise\n  \\(n+1-c\\) incidences.\n\n* a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\;(2\\le c\\le n+1)\\) contains the points\n  \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); it contributes \\(c-1\\) incidences.\n\nLet \\(h\\), \\(v\\) and \\(d\\) be the numbers of horizontal, vertical and\ndiagonal lines that appear in the configuration; thus\n\\(s=h+v+d\\).  Summing the contributions we obtain  \n\n\\[\nI=\\sum_{c=1}^{n}(n+1-c)\\,(\\text{horizontal at }c)\n   +\\sum_{c=1}^{n}(n+1-c)\\,(\\text{vertical at }c)\n   +\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)\\,(\\text{diagonal at }c).\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nEach term \\((n+1-c)\\) or \\((c-1)\\) is an integer of the same parity as\n\\(c\\).  Consequently the right\u2013hand side of (2.2) is congruent modulo\n\\(2\\) to the total number of non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. to \\(s\\).\n\nFrom (2.1) we have  \n\n\\[\nI\\equiv 3\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\equiv n\\pmod 2,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) is an integer and \\(3\\equiv1\\pmod2\\).\nHence \\(s\\equiv n\\pmod2\\), proving the lemma. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary 2.2  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in a feasible configuration.\nSince the total number of lines equals \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nk=n-s\\equiv n-n\\equiv0\\pmod2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nk\\equiv n-(n-1)\\equiv1\\pmod2 .\n\\]\n\nThus either \\(k=0\\) or \\(k\\) is odd.  Moreover \\(k\\le n\\) because there\nare only \\(n\\) lines in total. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Sufficiency\n\nWe prove that every integer \\(k\\) described in Corollary\u202f2.2 can indeed\noccur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.3 (Base cases)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(\\mathcal T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=2\\) cover the set.\n\n* \\(k=1\\): keep the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\) and add any sunny\n  line through the remaining point \\((1,2)\\) (for instance the line\n  \\(y=2x\\)).  The three lines are distinct and satisfy the requirements. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.4 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) and for every admissible\n\\(k\\) (i.e. \\(k=0\\) or \\(k\\) odd with \\(k\\le n\\)) there exists a\nconfiguration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions.\nThen the same statement holds for \\(n+2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(k\\) be admissible for \\(n+2\\).\n\n*If \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal T_{\\,n+2}\\) has its first coordinate in\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,n+2\\}\\); hence it lies on one of the chosen lines.\nAll lines are non\u2011sunny, and we have exactly \\(n+2\\) of them.\n\n*If \\(k\\ge1\\) (so \\(k\\) is odd).*\nWrite \\(k=2t+1\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\) and \\(2t+1\\le n+2\\).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis there exists a configuration\n\\(\\mathcal C_n\\) for the triangle \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) that uses exactly\n\\(2t-1\\) sunny lines (the number \\(2t-1\\) is odd and does not exceed\n\\(n\\)).  Denote by \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) the set of its \\(n\\) lines.\n\nWe enlarge \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) to a configuration for \\(\\mathcal T_{\\,n+2}\\).\n\n1. **Three new non\u2011sunny border lines**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;x=n+1,\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\;y=n+1,\\qquad\n   \\ell_3:\\;x+y=n+3 .\n   \\tag{2.3}\n   \\]\n\n   Each of them lies completely inside the enlarged triangle and together\n   contain every point of \\(\\mathcal T_{\\,n+2}\\setminus\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n2. **Two new sunny lines**  \n\n   Consider the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1=(n+2,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,n+2),\n   \\tag{2.4}\n   \\]\n\n   which belong to the new border.  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\text{ the line through }P_1\\text{ and }(n,2),\\qquad\n   L_2:\\text{ the line through }P_2\\text{ and }(2,n).\n   \\tag{2.5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slopes of \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are respectively\n   \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{2-1}{n-(n+2)}=-\\frac1{2}\\) and\n   \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n- (n+2)}{2-1}=-2\\); neither equals \\(0\\), \\(-1\\)\n   nor is infinite.  Hence both lines are sunny.  Moreover each of them\n   passes through exactly one of the points \\(P_1,P_2\\) that are not\n   already covered by the three border lines.\n\n3. **Forming