\) then it also holds for \\(n+2\\).  Consequently, by\ninduction, the statement is true for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2.1 and Corollary\u202f2.2 we have proved that any feasible\nconfiguration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ odd},\\;k\\le n .\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2.5 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) obeying (2.7) there exists a\nconfiguration of \\(n\\) distinct lines meeting the two conditions of the\nproblem.  Hence (2.7) characterises precisely the set of admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0\\}\\cup\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid k\\text{ odd},\\;k\\le n\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. \u220e"
