iagonal, and every point it contained is still on another line of the same direction that remains in the family \u2026\u201d\n  * **Issue:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 The argument that \u201canother line of the same direction \u2026 remains in the family\u201d is not proved; it assumes the existence of a suitable replacement line without demonstrating that such a line is indeed present in the configuration after the deletions.\n\n* **Location:** Overall conclusion \u2013 \u201c\\(k=0\\) or \\(k\\) odd, \\(k\\le n\\) are exactly the admissible values.\u201d\n  * **Issue:** **Critical Error** \u2013 Because the foundational parity result is false, the final characterisation of admissible \\(k\\) is unsupported. In fact, for \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=2\\) (even) is realizable, contradicting the claimed description.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Verification Log\n\n### 1. Definition of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and terminology\nThe introduction of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and the distinction between sunny and non\u2011sunny lines are correct and clear.\n\n### 2. Lemma\u202f2.1 (Parity)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) lies on exactly three non\u2011sunny lines: the vertical line \\(x=a\\), the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\). Hence the total number of incidences between points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and non\u2011sunny lines equals  \n\\(I=3|\\mathcal T_n|=3\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe three lines mentioned are *geometric* lines that pass through the point, but the problem does **not** require those lines to belong to the chosen configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines. The configuration may contain sunny lines that also pass through the point, and it may omit one or more of the three non\u2011sunny lines. Therefore the number of incidences **between points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and the non\u2011sunny lines that are actually used** is **not** forced to be \\(3|\\mathcal T_n|\\). The equality is false in general, so the subsequent parity computation is based on an incorrect counting argument.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the step invalidates the entire Lemma.\n\n### 3. Consequence of Lemma\u202f2.1 \u2013 Corollary\u202f2.2\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cThus either \\(k=0\\) or \\(k\\) is odd. Moreover \\(k\\le n\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nCorollary\u202f2.2 is derived directly from Lemma\u202f2.1 (using \\(k=n-s\\)). Since Lemma\u202f2.1 is false, the parity restriction on \\(k\\) has no justification. In particular, for \\(n=3\\) a configuration with \\(k=2\\) sunny lines exists (e.g., \\(\\{y=1,\\;y=2x,\\;y=3x\\}\\)), disproving the claim that only \\(k=0\\) or odd \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error (propagated from Lemma\u202f2.1).\n\n### 4. Lemma\u202f2.3 (Base cases)\n\nThe lemma correctly lists the points of \\(\\mathcal T_3\\) and shows constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\). However, it **fails to mention** that \\(k=2\\) is also feasible, which contradicts the claimed \u201cadmissible values are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\)\u201d. This omission is a symptom of the earlier parity mistake.\n\n**Classification:** Justification Gap \u2013 the statement \u201cadmissible values are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\)\u201d is unsupported; a counter\u2011example exists.\n\n### 5. Lemma\u202f2.4 (Inductive step)\n\n#### 5.1 Claim that a configuration for any \\(n\\) contains at least three non\u2011sunny lines\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201c\u2026 such a choice is always possible because \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) contains at least three non\u2011sunny lines (the three directions are present in any covering of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\)).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nA covering need not contain a line of each of the three prohibited directions. The example \\(\\{y=1,\\;y=2x,\\;y=3x\\}\\) for \\(n=3\\) covers all required points while having only one non\u2011sunny line. Hence the assertion that \u201cthe three directions are present in any covering\u201d is false.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the inductive construction relies on the ability to delete three non\u2011sunny lines, which may be impossible.\n\n#### 5.2 Argument that deleting a non\u2011sunny line does not destroy coverage\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201c\u2026 each deleted line is either horizontal, vertical or diagonal, and every point it contained is still on another line of the same direction that remains in the family (for instance, if we delete the horizontal line \\(y=c\\) we keep the diagonal line \\(x+y=c+1\\) which also passes through all points with ordinate \\(c\\) \u2026).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe existence of a \u201creplacement\u201d line of the same direction is assumed without proof. The configuration after deletion may not contain any line of that direction; the argument does not guarantee that such a line is present. Therefore the claim is not rigorously justified.\n\n**Classification:** Justification Gap.\n\n### 6. Lemma\u202f2.5 (Existence for all admissible \\(k\\))\n\nSince Lemma\u202f2.4 is flawed, the induction that \u201cif the statement holds for \\(n\\) then it holds for \\(n+2\\)\u201d is not established. Consequently Lemma\u202f2.5 does not follow.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error (depends on previous invalid steps).\n\n### 7. Final conclusion (Equation\u202f(2.7))\n\nThe final statement \u201c\\(k=0\\) or \\(k\\) odd, \\(k\\le n\\)\u201d is unsupported because the parity restriction derived from Lemma\u202f2.1 is false, and the inductive construction intended to show sufficiency is invalid.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\nThe solution\u2019s core parity argument (Lemma\u202f2.1) is fundamentally incorrect, leading to an erroneous restriction on the possible numbers of sunny lines. Subsequent lemmas rely on this false premise and contain additional unjustified assumptions (e.g., the guaranteed presence of three non\u2011sunny lines). Therefore the presented solution d