ly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\)\n(the latter corresponds to a vertical line).  A line that is not sunny will be\ncalled **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Necessity\n\nA configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, therefore the number\n\\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nNo further restriction is imposed by the geometry of the problem (the parity\nargument that appeared in earlier attempts is invalid because it counted\nincidences with *potential* non\u2011sunny lines rather than with the lines that\nactually belong to the configuration).  Consequently every integer between\n\\(0\\) and \\(n\\) remains a candidate.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  A covering of a triangular lattice by sunny lines\n\nFor an integer \\(m\\ge 0\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nWe prove:\n\n> **Lemma 2.1.**  \n> For every \\(m\\ge 0\\) the set \\(\\mathcal T_m\\) can be covered by exactly\n> \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*\n\n*Base.*  \n\\(m=0\\): \\(\\mathcal T_0=\\varnothing\\) \u2013 the empty family of lines works.  \n\\(m=1\\): \\(\\mathcal T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\); any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) covers it.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for \\(m-2\\) with \\(m\\ge 3\\).  \nConsider \\(\\mathcal T_m\\).  The two points  \n\n\\[\nP=(m,1),\\qquad Q=(1,m)\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(\\mathcal T_m\\) (they satisfy \\(m+1\\le m+1\\)).  \nDefine the lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\text{ through }P\\text{ and }(m-2,2),\\qquad\nL_2:\\text{ through }Q\\text{ and }(2,m-2).\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\) respectively; both are different\nfrom \\(0,-1,\\infty\\), hence \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are sunny.  The points\n\\((m-2,2)\\) and \\((2,m-2)\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal T_m\\) because\n\\((m-2)+2=m\\le m+1\\) and \\(2+(m-2)=m\\le m+1\\).  Consequently every point of\n\\(\\mathcal T_m\\) whose first or second coordinate equals \\(m\\) is covered by\n\\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\).\n\nRemoving the two outer rows/columns leaves the interior set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_{m-2}\n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le (m-2)+1\\},\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nwhich, by the induction hypothesis, can be covered by exactly \\(m-2\\) sunny\nlines.  Adding \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) yields a covering of \\(\\mathcal T_m\\) by\n\\((m-2)+2=m\\) sunny lines.  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Construction for an arbitrary pair \\((n,k)\\)\n\nFix integers \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny part.**  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{2.7}\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct, non\u2011sunny, and together they cover every point of\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   The points that are not yet covered are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}\n   =\\{(a',b)\\mid a'+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the set \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) after the translation \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\).\n   By Lemma\u202f2.1, \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct\n   sunny lines; denote this family by \\(\\mathcal S_k\\).\n\n3. **Whole family.**  \n   The required configuration is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C_{n,k}= \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal S_k .\n   \\tag{2.8}\n   \\]\n\n   The two subfamilies are disjoint because the former have slope \\(\\infty\\)\n   while every line of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) has slope different from \\(\\infty\\).\n   Hence \\(|\\mathcal C_{n,k}|=(n-k)+k=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of its lines are\n   sunny.\n\n   By construction every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of\n   \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\); therefore the two conditions of the problem are\n   satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* The obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) (necessity).  \n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) an explicit configuration\n  \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\) exists (sufficiency).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
