atements are correct.\n\n2. **Claim about coverage of points with first or second coordinate equal to \\(m\\).**  \n   - The only points of \\(\\mathcal T_m\\) with first coordinate \\(m\\) are \\((m,1)\\); with second coordinate \\(m\\) are \\((1,m)\\). Both are on \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) respectively.  \n   - The claim is accurate, though trivial.\n\n3. **Removal of \u201ctwo outer rows/columns\u201d.**  \n   - The solution asserts that after deleting the points with first coordinate \\(m\\) or second coordinate \\(m\\) we obtain the set \\(\\mathcal T_{m-2}\\) (equation\u202f(2.6)).  \n   - **Error:**  \n     \\[\n     \\mathcal T_m\\setminus\\{(m,b),(a,m)\\mid a,b\\ge1\\}\n     =\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a\\le m-1,\\ b\\le m-1,\\ a+b\\le m+1\\},\n     \\]\n     which is **strictly larger** than \\(\\mathcal T_{m-2}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le m-1\\}\\).  \n     For example, with \\(m=5\\) the point \\((4,2)\\) belongs to the remaining set (since \\(4+2=6\\le6\\)) but does **not** belong to \\(\\mathcal T_{3}\\) (which requires sum \\(\\le4\\)).  \n   - Therefore the equality claimed in (2.6) is false. The induction hypothesis (that the interior can be covered by \\(m-2\\) sunny lines) cannot be applied to the actual interior set.\n\n4. **Consequent conclusion.**  \n   - Because the interior set is not \\(\\mathcal T_{m-2}\\), the step \u201cby the induction hypothesis, it can be covered by exactly \\(m-2\\) sunny lines\u201d is unjustified. This invalidates the inductive proof of Lemma\u202f2.1.\n\n**Classification:** This is a **Critical Error**: the lemma\u2019s proof collapses, and the statement of the lemma remains unproved.\n\n### 2.3 Construction for arbitrary \\((n,k)\\)\n\n- The construction uses Lemma\u202f2.1 to cover the translated set \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (family \\(\\mathcal S_k\\)).  \n- Since Lemma\u202f2.1 has not been established, the existence of such a family \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) is not guaranteed. Consequently the whole sufficiency argument rests on an unproved claim.  \n- This propagates the critical error from Lemma\u202f2.1 to the final answer.\n\n### 2.4 Conclusion\n- The final statement \u201cthe admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(\\{0,1,\\dots,n\\}\\)\u201d is not justified because the construction for \\(k>0\\) lacks a valid proof.  \n- Therefore the solution is **invalid**.\n\n--- \n\n**Overall assessment:** The solution fails to rigorously establish the key Lemma\u202f2.1 due to an incorrect identification of the interior set after removing outer rows/columns. This critical flaw undermines the entire sufficiency part of the argument, rendering the claimed answer unproven. Consequently, the solution is not correct."
