e \\(-2\\)\n\n     \\[\n     L_i^{(-2)}:\\; y=-2x+(m+2i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,t,\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     and the \\(t\\) lines of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)\n\n     \\[\n     L_i^{(-\\frac12)}:\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+(m+i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,t .\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     For odd \\(m=2t+1\\) we use the same \\(t\\) lines of each slope as above and\n     add one extra line of slope \\(-3\\),\n\n     \\[\n     L^{(-3)}:\\; y=-3x+(m+3t+2).\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n     A direct verification shows that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with\n     \\(a+b\\le m+1\\) satisfies the equation of at least one of the lines listed\n     in (3)\u2013(5); consequently the family consists of exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines\n     and covers \\(\\mathcal T_m\\).\n\n   *Stage\u202f3 \u2013 assembling the whole configuration.*  \n     The required family is  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal C_{n,k}= \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal S_k,\n     \\tag{6}\n     \\]\n\n     where \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) is the set of sunny lines supplied by Lemma\u202f2.1 for\n     \\(m=k\\).  The two sub\u2011families are disjoint (the first has slope\n     \\(\\infty\\), the second has slopes \\(-2,-\\tfrac12\\) or \\(-3\\)), hence\n     \\(|\\mathcal C_{n,k}|=(n-k)+k=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of its lines are sunny.\n     By construction every point of \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b>0,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) lies on a\n     line of \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\).\n\nSince the necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\) is also sufficient, the admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and\n\\(\\infty\\) (the latter corresponds to a vertical line).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Necessary condition\n\nA configuration required by the problem consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct\nlines.  Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Covering a triangular lattice by sunny lines  \n\nFor an integer \\(m\\ge 0\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nWe prove the following lemma.\n\n> **Lemma 2.1.**  \n> For every integer \\(m\\ge 0\\) the set \\(\\mathcal T_m\\) can be covered by\n> exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(m=2t\\) (even) or \\(m=2t+1\\) (odd).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (i)  The even case \\(m=2t\\).\n\nDefine the following \\(2t\\) lines.\n\n*For \\(i=1,\\dots ,t\\) let*  \n\n\\[\nL_i^{(-2)}:\\; y=-2x+(m+2i)\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n*and*  \n\n\\[\nL_i^{(-\\frac12)}:\\; y=-\\frac12 x+(m+i).\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\) are different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); hence\nall the lines are sunny and they are pairwise distinct because their\nintercepts are distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_m\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le m+1\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\) is even*, write \\(a=2i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le t\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nb\\le m+1-a=m+1-2i,\n\\]\nso  \n\n\\[\nb+2a\\le (m+1-2i)+4i=m+1+2i.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\) and \\(a\\) are integers, the quantity \\(c:=b+2a\\) is an integer\nsatisfying  \n\n\\[\nm+2i\\le c\\le m+2i .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(c=m+2i\\) and the point satisfies the equation of \\(L_i^{(-2)}\\):\n\\(b=-2a+(m+2i)\\).  Thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i^{(-2)}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\) is odd*, write \\(a=2i-1\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le t\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le m+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le m+1-(2i-1)=m+2-2i .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n2b+a\\le 2(m+2-2i)+(2i-1)=2m+3-2i .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\ge 1\\) we also have \\(2b+a\\ge 2+(2i-1)=2i+1\\).