nes and covers \\(\\mathcal T_m\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Construction for arbitrary \\((n,k)\\)\n\nFix integers \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny vertical lines.**  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{2.8}\n   \\]\n\n   They are non\u2011sunny and together cover every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\)\n   with \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n2. **Sunny lines covering the remaining triangle.**  \n   The points not yet covered are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\tag{2.9}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the triangular set of size \\(k\\).  \n   By Lemma\u202f2.1 there exists a family \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) of exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines that covers \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).\n\n3. **The whole family.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C_{n,k}= \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal S_k .\n   \\tag{2.10}\n   \\]\n\n   The two sub\u2011families are disjoint because the first consists of vertical\n   lines (slope \\(\\infty\\)) while every line in \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) has slope\n   \\(-2\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\) or \\(-3\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal C_{n,k}|=(n-k)+k=n ,\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of its lines are sunny.\n\n   Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) either has first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\) and\n   therefore lies on one of the vertical lines, or has first coordinate\n   \\(>n-k\\) and consequently belongs to \\(\\mathcal T_k\\), whence it lies on a\n   line of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\).  Thus the two required conditions of the problem\n   are satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown\n\n* the obvious necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\);\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) an explicit configuration of\n  \\(n\\) distinct lines exists that contains exactl