"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe problem is reduced to the following two steps.\n\n1. **Necessary condition.**  \n   Any configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, therefore the\n   number \\(k\\) of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Hence \\(k\\le n\\) is\n   necessary.\n\n2. **Sufficiency.**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   *Step\u202f2.1 \u2013 a non\u2011sunny \u201cbackbone\u201d.*  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{2.1}\n   \\]\n\n   They are not sunny and they cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with\n   \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f2.2 \u2013 covering the remaining triangular set.*  \n   After Step\u202f2.1 the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\tag{2.2}\n   \\]\n\n   a right\u2011angled integer triangle of \u201csize\u2019\u2019 \\(k\\).  \n   The crucial lemma is:\n\n   > **Lemma\u202f2.1.**  \n   > For every integer \\(k\\ge 0\\) the set \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) can be covered by\n   > exactly \\(k\\) distinct **sunny** lines.\n\n   *Proof of Lemma\u202f2.1.*  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=-2x+(k+i+1).\n   \\tag{2.3}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\); therefore\n   every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because their\n   intercepts \\(k+i+1\\) are distinct.\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_k\\).  Two cases are possible.\n\n   *Case\u202f1 \u2013 \\(a+b=k+1\\) (the hypotenuse).*\n   Then \\(b=k+1-a\\) and substituting in (2.3) with \\(i=a\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   -2a+(k+a+1)=k+1-a=b,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).\n\n   *Case\u202f2 \u2013 \\(a+b\\le k\\) (strictly inside the triangle).*\n   Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) we have \\(a\\le k\\).  Choose \\(i=a\\) again.\n   From (2.3) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   y=-2a+(k+a+1)=k+1-a .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a+b\\le k\\) we have \\(b\\le k-a\\), and consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\le k-a < k+1-a .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(b\\) is **strictly smaller** than the value of the right\u2011hand side\n   of (2.3) for \\(x=a\\).  Because the right\u2011hand side of (2.3) decreases by\n   exactly \\(2\\) when \\(x\\) is increased by \\(1\\), there exists a unique\n   integer \\(x_0\\) with \\(1\\le x_0\\le a\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   -2x_0+(k+a+1)=b .\n   \\tag{2.4}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (2.4) for \\(x_0\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   x_0=\\frac{k+a+1-b}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   The numerator is an integer because \\(k+a+1-b\\) has the same parity as\n   \\(k+1\\) (both are even: \\(k+a+1-b = (k+1)+(a-b)\\) and \\(a-b\\) has the\n   same parity as \\(k+1\\) because \\(a+b\\) and \\(k+1\\) have opposite parity).\n   Moreover \\(1\\le x_0\\le a\\) follows from \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\le k-a\\).\n   Therefore \\(x_0\\) is a legitimate integer abscissa, and (2.4) shows that\n   \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{x_0}\\).\n\n   In both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to one of the lines\n   \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\).  Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\) cover the\n   whole set \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).  \u220e\n\n   *Step\u202f2.3 \u2013 assembling the whole configuration.*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C_{n,k}= \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\},\n   \\tag{2.5}\n   \\]\n\n   where the \\(L_i\\) are the lines from Lemma\u202f2.1 with the same \\(k\\).\n   The first family consists of non\u2011sunny vertical lines, the second family\n   consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  The two families are disjoint,\n   therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal C_{n,k}|=(n-k)+k=n .\n   \\]\n\n   By construction the vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate\n   \\(\\le n-k\\); the remaining points correspond, after the translation\n   \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\), to the set \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) and are covered\n   by the lines \\(L_i\\).  Consequently every point \\((a,b)\\) with\n   \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line of\n   \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nSince the necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\) is also sufficient, the admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and\n\\(\\infty\\) (the latter corresponds to a vertical line).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Necessary condition\n\nAny admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the\nnumber \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Covering a right\u2011angled integer triangle by sunny lines  \n\nFor an integer \\(m\\ge 0\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nWe prove the following lemma.\n\n> **Lemma\u202f2.1.**  \n> For every integer \\(m\\ge 0\\) the set \\(\\mathcal T_m\\) can be covered by\n> exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=-2x+(m+i+1).\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\); hence each\n\\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(m\\) lines are distinct because their intercepts\n\\(m+i+1\\) are distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_m\\).  Write \\(s=a+b\\) (\\(1\\le s\\le m+1\\)).\n\n*If \\(s=m+1\\) (the hypotenuse).*  \nThen \\(b=m+1-a\\).  Substituting \\(i=a\\) in (2.9) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=-2a+(m+a+1)=m+1-a=b,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).\n\n*If \\(s\\le m\\) (strictly inside the triangle).*  \nBecause \\(a\\ge 1\\) we have \\(a\\le m\\).  Choose the integer  \n\n\\[\ni:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m+1-b}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\ge 1\\) we have \\(1\\le i\\le m\\).  From the definition of \\(i\\),\n\n\\[\n2i-1\\le m+1-b\\le 2i .\n\\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nRearranging the right\u2011hand inequality of (2.11) yields  \n\n\\[\nb\\le -2i+(m+i+1).\n\\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand inequality of (2.11) gives  \n\n\\[\nb\\ge -2(i-1)+(m+(i-1)+1)= -2i+(m+i+1)+2 .\n\\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b\\) is either equal to \\(-2i+(m+i+1)\\) or exceeds it by exactly\n\\(1\\).  The latter cannot happen because the right\u2011hand side of (2.9) is an\ninteger while the left\u2011hand side \\(b\\) is also an integer; the difference\nbetween the two sides of (2.12) is at most \\(1\\), so equality must hold.\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nb=-2i+(m+i+1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\).  Hence every point of\n\\(\\mathcal T_m\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Construction for arbitrary \\((n,k)\\)\n\nFix integers \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny vertical lines.**  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{2.14}\n   \\]\n\n   They are not sunny and together cover every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\)\n   with \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n2. **Sunny lines covering the remaining triangle.**  \n   After Step\u202f1 the uncovered points are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\tag{2.15}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the right\u2011angled integer triangle of size \\(k\\).  \n   By Lemma\u202f2.1 there exist exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover\n   \\(\\mathcal T_k\\); denote this family by \\(\\mathcal S_k\\).\n\n3. **The whole family.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C_{n,k}= \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal S_k .\n   \\tag{2.16}\n   \\]\n\n   The two sub\u2011families are disjoint because the first consists of vertical\n   lines (slope \\(\\infty\\)) while every line in \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) has slope\n   \\(-2\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal C_{n,k}|=(n-k)+k=n ,\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of its lines are sunny.\n\n   By construction the vertical lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with\n   first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\); the remaining points correspond, after the\n   translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\), to the set \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) and\n   are covered by the sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\).  Therefore every\n   point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line\n   of \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\) is also sufficient, because for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the explicit configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\)\ndescribed above satisfies both requirements of the problem.\n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]  \n\n\u220e"
