\\(s=a+b\\) (\\(1\\le s\\le m+1\\)).\n\n*If \\(s=m+1\\) (the hypotenuse).*  \nThen \\(b=m+1-a\\).  Substituting \\(i=a\\) in (2.9) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=-2a+(m+a+1)=m+1-a=b,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).\n\n*If \\(s\\le m\\) (strictly inside the triangle).*  \nBecause \\(a\\ge 1\\) we have \\(a\\le m\\).  Choose the integer  \n\n\\[\ni:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m+1-b}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\ge 1\\) we have \\(1\\le i\\le m\\).  From the definition of \\(i\\),\n\n\\[\n2i-1\\le m+1-b\\le 2i .\n\\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nRearranging the right\u2011hand inequality of (2.11) yields  \n\n\\[\nb\\le -2i+(m+i+1).\n\\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand inequality of (2.11) gives  \n\n\\[\nb\\ge -2(i-1)+(m+(i-1)+1)= -2i+(m+i+1)+2 .\n\\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b\\) is either equal to \\(-2i+(m+i+1)\\) or exceeds it by exactly\n\\(1\\).  The latter cannot happen because the right\u2011hand side of (2.9) is an\ninteger while the left\u2011hand side \\(b\\) is also an integer; the difference\nbetween the two sides of (2.12) is at most \\(1\\), so equality must hold.\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nb=-2i+(m+i+1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\).  Hence every point of\n\\(\\mathcal T_m\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Construction for arbitrary \\((n,k)\\)\n\nFix integers \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny vertical lines.**  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{2.14}\n   \\]\n\n   They are not sunny and together cover every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\)\n   with \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n2. **Sunny lines covering the remaining triangle.**  \n   After Step\u202f1 the uncovered points are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\tag{2.15}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the right\u2011angled integer triangle of size \\(k\\).  \n   By Lemma\u202f2.1 there exist exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover\n   \\(\\mathcal T_k\\); denote this family by \\(\\mathcal S_k\\).\n\n3. **The whole family.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C_{n,k}= \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal S_k .\n   \\tag{2.16}\n   \\]\n\n   The two sub\u2011families are disjoint because the first consists of vertical\n   lines (slope \\(\\infty\\)) while every line in \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) has slope\n   \\(-2\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal C_{n,k}|=(n-k)+k=n ,\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of its lines are sunny.\n\n   By construction the vertical lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with\n   first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\); the remaining points correspond, after the\n   translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\), to the set \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) and\n   are covered by the sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\).  Therefore every\n   point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line\n   of \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\) is also sufficient, because for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the explicit configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\)\ndescribed above satisfies both requirements of the problem.\n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]  \n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
