rbitrary \\(k\\) (including \\(k=n\\)) lacks a valid foundation.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Verification Log\n\n### Preliminary Definitions\nThe solution defines  \n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\nand calls a line *sunny* iff its slope is not \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\).  \nThese definitions are correct.\n\n---\n\n### 2.1 Necessary condition\nThe statement \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) follows directly from \u201cexactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny\u201d.  \n**Correct.**\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n1. **Identification of collinear triples** \u2013 The solution correctly notes that the only triples of points in \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) that are collinear have slopes \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\).  \n   **Correct.**\n\n2. **Claim:** \u201ca sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\).\u201d  \n   This follows from the previous observation.  \n   **Correct.**\n\n3. **Bounding the number of points covered by sunny lines:**  \n   With \\(k\\) sunny lines, at most \\(2k\\) points can be covered.  \n   **Correct.**\n\n4. **Analysis of non\u2011sunny lines:**  \n   The solution argues that a vertical line can contain at most three points, and at most one non\u2011sunny line can be vertical because otherwise two vertical lines would coincide.  \n   The statement \u201cotherwise two vertical lines would coincide\u201d is **false**: distinct vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, so they are distinct (e.g., \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\)). Hence any number of vertical non\u2011sunny lines can be used, provided their \\(x\\)-coordinates are different.  \n   **Critical Error.** This mistake leads to an underestimate of the covering power of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n5. **Incorrect restriction on simultaneous use of a vertical and a horizontal line:**  \n   The solution says \u201cthe three\u2011point vertical line and the three\u2011point horizontal line cannot appear simultaneously (they would share the point \\((1,1)\\))\u201d.  \n   Overlap of lines at a point is perfectly allowed; the problem only requires distinct lines, not disjoint point sets.  \n   **Critical Error.**\n\n6. **Conclusion that the maximum attainable total is five points:**  \n   Because of the previous erroneous restrictions, the author claims that no configuration can cover all six points when \\(k\\ge1\\).  \n   **Counterexample:**  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   x=1 &\\text{(vertical, non\u2011sunny)}\\\\\n   y=1 &\\text{(horizontal, non\u2011sunny)}\\\\\n   y=x &\\text{(slope }1,\\ \\text{sunny)}\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   These three distinct lines cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\). Hence a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists, contradicting the claim that only \\(k=0\\) is possible.  \n   **Critical Error.**\n\nBecause the reasoning for \\(n=3\\) is fundamentally flawed, the derived set of admissible \\(k\\) for \\(n=3\\) is incorrect.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 Lemma\u202f2.1 (covering a right\u2011angled integer triangle)\n\n**Statement of Lemma:** \u201cFor every integer \\(m\\ge0\\) the set \\(\\mathcal T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines.\u201d\n\nThe proof proceeds by induction.\n\n1. **Base cases \\(m=0,1,2\\):**  \n   - \\(m=0\\) is trivial.  \n   - \\(m=1\\): the line \\(y=2x-1\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and has slope \\(2\\) (sunny). Correct.  \n   - \\(m=2\\): the author attempts to give two sunny lines, but the description is contradictory. The line \\(L_1: y=2x-1\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,3)\\); the line \\(L_2: y=-2x+5\\) passes through \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\). Neither line contains the point \\((1,2)\\). The author then says \u201cthe point \\((1,2)\\) lies on neither line, but it is covered by \\(L_2\\) after a translation of the whole picture\u201d, which is a hand\u2011wavy justification that does **not** actually produce a covering of the required set.  \n   **Justification Gap** (the base case \\(m=2\\) is not rigorously established).\n\n2. **Inductive step (equations (2.6)\u2013(2.9)):**  \n   - The line \\(L_1: y=-2x+(m+3)\\) indeed has slope \\(-2\\) (sunny).  \n   - The claim that for each integer  \n     \\[\n     t\\in\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n     \\]  \n     the point \\((t,\\,m+3-2t)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal T_m\\) is **false**.  \n     For example, with \\(m=4\\) and \\(t=1\\) we obtain the point \\((1,5)\\) whose sum of coordinates is \\(6>m+1=5\\); thus it is **outside** \\(\\mathcal T_4\\). In general, the sum of coordinates of \\((t,m+3-2t)\\) equals \\(m+3-t\\), which exceeds \\(m+1\\) for all \\(t\\le2\\). Consequently the set of points claimed to be covered by \\(L_1\\) is not a subset of \\(\\mathcal T_m\\).  \n   **Critical Error.**\n\n   - Because the claimed coverage of the \u201csouth\u2011west diagonal\u201d is incorrect, the reduction from \\(\\mathcal T_m\\) to \\(\\mathcal T_{m-2}\\) does not hold. The inductive argument collapses.\n\nThus Lemma\u202f2.1 is not proved; in fact the construction given is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4 Construction for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nThe construction relies on two ingredients:\n\n1. **A vertical \u201cbackbone\u201d** consisting of the lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   This part is correct: these lines are non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\).\n\n2. **Covering the remaining triangle \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines** (Step\u202f3).  \n   The author invokes Lemma\u202f2.1 for this purpose. Since Lemma\u202f2.1 is false, the existence of such a sunny covering for arbitrary \\(k\\) is not established.  \n\n   - For small values of \\(k\\) (e.g., \\(k=1,2,3\\)) one can manually produce sunny coverings, but the claim that **every** \\(k\\) up to \\(n\\) works lacks justification.  \n   - Moreover, the case \\(k=n\\) would require covering the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(n\\) sunny lines and no non\u2011sunny lines. The solution provides no rigorous argument that this is always possible.\n\nBecause a crucial component of the construction is unproved, the conclusion that \u201cfor every \\(n\\ge4\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a required configuration exists\u201d is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5 Final answer\n\nThe final answer lists the admissible \\(k\\) values based on the previous (flawed) analysis. Since the analysis for \\(n=3\\) is incorrect and the general construction for \\(n\\ge4\\) rests on an invalid lemma, the answer cannot be considered correct.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\nThe solution contains multiple **Critical Errors**:\n\n* Misinterpretation of the problem\u2019s allowance for overlapping lines, leading to an erroneous impossibility claim for \\(n=3\\).\n* A false combinatorial bound for the case \\(n=3\\) and an incorrect conclusion that only \\(k=0\\) is possible.\n* An invalid inductive construction in Lemma\u202f2.1 (incorrect claim about points belonging to \\(\\mathcal T_m\\) and a flawed reduction step).\n* Reliance on the invalid lemma for the general construction, leaving the existence of configurations for many \\(k\\) unproved.\n\nConsequently, the solution does **not** correctly solve the problem."
